WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.460 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:02.460 --> 00:00:03.444 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:03.444 --> 00:00:06.888 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:06.888 --> 00:00:16.713 align:middle line:90%


00:00:16.713 --> 00:00:17.380 align:middle line:90%
JEAN TIROLE: OK.

00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:21.160 align:middle line:84%
So today, we are going
to cover a topic which

00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:26.560 align:middle line:90%
is quite unusual for 14.271.

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:28.550 align:middle line:84%
Nonetheless, it's
quite important.

00:00:28.550 --> 00:00:32.180 align:middle line:84%
It's about the views
of people about--

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:36.190 align:middle line:84%
that people have about
the morality of markets.

00:00:36.190 --> 00:00:38.810 align:middle line:84%
[14.]271 is about
antitrust mainly.

00:00:38.810 --> 00:00:41.740 align:middle line:84%
And of course, antitrust
is about market.

00:00:41.740 --> 00:00:45.820 align:middle line:84%
And if people don't
believe in markets,

00:00:45.820 --> 00:00:48.280 align:middle line:84%
then that raises
issues about what we

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:50.495 align:middle line:90%
are doing in the first place.

00:00:50.495 --> 00:00:53.120 align:middle line:84%
Furthermore, there are lots of
development nowadays-- actually,

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:58.150 align:middle line:84%
it's broader than antitrust--
about what independent agencies

00:00:58.150 --> 00:00:59.680 align:middle line:90%
should be doing.

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:03.070 align:middle line:84%
So that has started
with central banks.

00:01:03.070 --> 00:01:05.950 align:middle line:84%
Their mission of enlarge
is no longer just

00:01:05.950 --> 00:01:09.310 align:middle line:84%
inflation targeting and
financial stability,

00:01:09.310 --> 00:01:11.210 align:middle line:90%
but it goes way beyond.

00:01:11.210 --> 00:01:14.530 align:middle line:84%
And now everybody talks
about green central banks.

00:01:14.530 --> 00:01:18.530 align:middle line:84%
And there is a issue about
whether fighting the climate

00:01:18.530 --> 00:01:20.570 align:middle line:84%
change should be
the responsibility

00:01:20.570 --> 00:01:22.820 align:middle line:84%
of the central bank or it
should be the responsibility

00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:25.460 align:middle line:90%
of ministries.

00:01:25.460 --> 00:01:28.310 align:middle line:84%
With antitrust, it's a little
bit the same because there are

00:01:28.310 --> 00:01:31.040 align:middle line:84%
more and more discussions--
in particular, in Europe,

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:32.690 align:middle line:90%
but all over the world--

00:01:32.690 --> 00:01:34.910 align:middle line:84%
about whether the
consumer standard

00:01:34.910 --> 00:01:36.510 align:middle line:90%
is the right way to proceed.

00:01:36.510 --> 00:01:39.080 align:middle line:84%
So the consumer
standard is basically

00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:43.310 align:middle line:84%
that the antitrust mission
is to defend the consumer.

00:01:43.310 --> 00:01:45.350 align:middle line:84%
Maybe not the
short-term consumer.

00:01:45.350 --> 00:01:49.280 align:middle line:84%
Maybe an intertemporal
consumer because sometimes you

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:51.360 align:middle line:84%
have tradeoffs between
today and tomorrow.

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:55.760 align:middle line:84%
But by and large, the mission
of antitrust authorities

00:01:55.760 --> 00:01:58.890 align:middle line:84%
everywhere in the world
is to defend the consumer.

00:01:58.890 --> 00:02:00.890 align:middle line:90%
It's a pretty clear mission.

00:02:00.890 --> 00:02:04.760 align:middle line:84%
It's difficult in practice,
but on paper, it's simple.

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:09.419 align:middle line:84%
You just stand as an
advocate for the consumer.

00:02:09.419 --> 00:02:11.450 align:middle line:84%
But now people say,
no, no, no, maybe we

00:02:11.450 --> 00:02:15.980 align:middle line:84%
should relax antitrust
first, for example,

00:02:15.980 --> 00:02:17.930 align:middle line:90%
when we choose cases.

00:02:17.930 --> 00:02:22.960 align:middle line:84%
So if there is a caviar
cartel and a milk cartel,

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:25.120 align:middle line:84%
then if you have
unlimited resources,

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:28.060 align:middle line:84%
you first go after
the milk cartel.

00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:31.480 align:middle line:84%
And the caviar cartel,
you just say, OK, this

00:02:31.480 --> 00:02:32.750 align:middle line:90%
is not that important.

00:02:32.750 --> 00:02:35.530 align:middle line:84%
So we should take into
account redistribution.

00:02:35.530 --> 00:02:38.440 align:middle line:84%
We should take into
account climate change.

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:42.010 align:middle line:84%
So if we have a cartel
which actually promotes

00:02:42.010 --> 00:02:43.780 align:middle line:84%
the fight against
climate change,

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:46.660 align:middle line:84%
maybe we should tolerate the
cartel and so on and so forth.

00:02:46.660 --> 00:02:50.600 align:middle line:84%
There are lots, lots of
discussions about that.

00:02:50.600 --> 00:02:54.130 align:middle line:84%
Actually, next week, I'm going
to a conference organizing

00:02:54.130 --> 00:02:56.120 align:middle line:90%
by Margrethe Vestager.

00:02:56.120 --> 00:02:57.850 align:middle line:90%
I'm giving a keynote there.

00:02:57.850 --> 00:03:02.290 align:middle line:84%
And the panel just after me
is exactly about this topic,

00:03:02.290 --> 00:03:05.490 align:middle line:84%
with people like Thomas
Philippon and others.

00:03:05.490 --> 00:03:07.820 align:middle line:90%
It's really about this topic.

00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:10.000 align:middle line:84%
So I will know more
in a week from now.

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:12.590 align:middle line:84%
It's not going to help
very much for today.

00:03:12.590 --> 00:03:15.430 align:middle line:84%
But that's really
important because,

00:03:15.430 --> 00:03:17.970 align:middle line:90%
in the end, that's the mission.

00:03:17.970 --> 00:03:22.190 align:middle line:84%
In my own view, I've worked
on mission and advocacy

00:03:22.190 --> 00:03:23.630 align:middle line:90%
with Mathias Dewatripont.

00:03:23.630 --> 00:03:25.730 align:middle line:84%
And my own view is
that it's not always

00:03:25.730 --> 00:03:29.810 align:middle line:84%
a good idea to
have mission creep.

00:03:29.810 --> 00:03:32.790 align:middle line:84%
And if the issues are real--
so inequality is real,

00:03:32.790 --> 00:03:35.090 align:middle line:84%
climate change is
real, of course--

00:03:35.090 --> 00:03:39.080 align:middle line:84%
maybe it's not, say, to
the antitrust authority

00:03:39.080 --> 00:03:42.530 align:middle line:84%
to tackle them, but actually
more political bodies

00:03:42.530 --> 00:03:44.580 align:middle line:84%
should be doing
that as opposed to--

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:48.800 align:middle line:84%
but that's my own
view on those issues.

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:53.450 align:middle line:84%
But there is a broader thing
about whether we like markets.

00:03:53.450 --> 00:03:59.750 align:middle line:84%
And there is this bizarre
thing that almost every economy

00:03:59.750 --> 00:04:02.330 align:middle line:84%
in the world is
a market economy,

00:04:02.330 --> 00:04:04.910 align:middle line:84%
and nonetheless,
nobody likes markets.

00:04:04.910 --> 00:04:08.960 align:middle line:90%
So it's kind of paradox somehow.

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:15.380 align:middle line:84%
And I will come back
to the big picture

00:04:15.380 --> 00:04:19.110 align:middle line:84%
about how we think about
the organization of society.

00:04:19.110 --> 00:04:22.320 align:middle line:90%
But it's kind of strange.

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:25.130 align:middle line:84%
So what I'm going to do is
to have a brief introduction

00:04:25.130 --> 00:04:28.010 align:middle line:84%
about this and then
switch to a paper

00:04:28.010 --> 00:04:30.170 align:middle line:84%
that I'm finishing--
the revision I'm

00:04:30.170 --> 00:04:32.690 align:middle line:84%
finishing with
Mathias Dewatripont

00:04:32.690 --> 00:04:34.290 align:middle line:90%
on the morality of markets.

00:04:34.290 --> 00:04:37.140 align:middle line:84%
But I just want to have
a broad picture first.

00:04:37.140 --> 00:04:39.920 align:middle line:84%
I will go relatively
fast because it's

00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:43.920 align:middle line:84%
developed, in some detail, in
Economics for the Common Good,

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:47.180 align:middle line:84%
if you have seen Economics
for the Common Good, the book.

00:04:47.180 --> 00:04:51.950 align:middle line:84%
But feel free to ask
questions, actually.

00:04:51.950 --> 00:04:55.172 align:middle line:84%
Try to ask more questions
than you did on Monday.

00:04:55.172 --> 00:04:56.005 align:middle line:90%
That's your mission.

00:04:56.005 --> 00:04:59.480 align:middle line:90%


00:04:59.480 --> 00:05:03.950 align:middle line:84%
There are two kinds of
attacks against markets.

00:05:03.950 --> 00:05:07.370 align:middle line:84%
The first is that a number
of markets are what's called

00:05:07.370 --> 00:05:08.750 align:middle line:90%
"repugnant."

00:05:08.750 --> 00:05:14.990 align:middle line:84%
So we don't feel very much
at ease with those markets.

00:05:14.990 --> 00:05:21.180 align:middle line:84%
And almost every well-known
philosopher has a book on that.

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:24.160 align:middle line:84%
I put here Debra Satz
and Michael Sandel,

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:27.000 align:middle line:84%
but there are many other
well-known philosophers who also

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:29.070 align:middle line:90%
have books on--

00:05:29.070 --> 00:05:32.190 align:middle line:84%
with roughly the same
title, not the same content.

00:05:32.190 --> 00:05:35.946 align:middle line:84%
Because if you compare those
two books, for example,

00:05:35.946 --> 00:05:38.100 align:middle line:84%
they start from the
same point of view,

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:42.690 align:middle line:84%
but they have very
different analyses.

00:05:42.690 --> 00:05:44.040 align:middle line:90%
The most well-known one--

00:05:44.040 --> 00:05:49.440 align:middle line:84%
I mean, this was a huge
bestseller of Michael Sandel--

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:54.370 align:middle line:84%
talks about all those
markets that make us uneasy.

00:05:54.370 --> 00:05:58.920 align:middle line:84%
Do we have markets for
babies or whatever?

00:05:58.920 --> 00:06:04.830 align:middle line:84%
All those things are actually
creating some conflicts

00:06:04.830 --> 00:06:07.860 align:middle line:84%
in our brain about whether
we should have those markets.

00:06:07.860 --> 00:06:13.180 align:middle line:90%
Now, this is more specific.

00:06:13.180 --> 00:06:15.100 align:middle line:90%
It's not the market in general.

00:06:15.100 --> 00:06:19.060 align:middle line:84%
It's specific markets
that we don't quite like.

00:06:19.060 --> 00:06:22.500 align:middle line:90%
But the frontier is unclear.

00:06:22.500 --> 00:06:25.300 align:middle line:84%
So a number of people
think, for example,

00:06:25.300 --> 00:06:32.050 align:middle line:84%
that tradable emission permits
market, the ETS systems

00:06:32.050 --> 00:06:35.290 align:middle line:90%
for carbon, are immoral.

00:06:35.290 --> 00:06:39.580 align:middle line:84%
They are immoral because you can
pay for the right to pollute.

00:06:39.580 --> 00:06:41.320 align:middle line:90%
Or carbon tax is the same.

00:06:41.320 --> 00:06:43.840 align:middle line:84%
You pay the carbon tax,
and you pay to pollute.

00:06:43.840 --> 00:06:46.030 align:middle line:84%
Of course, you can
ask, is that more

00:06:46.030 --> 00:06:49.010 align:middle line:84%
moral to not have
to pay to pollute?

00:06:49.010 --> 00:06:52.640 align:middle line:84%
But then there is a
big silence in front.

00:06:52.640 --> 00:06:56.350 align:middle line:84%
But lots of people, in my
experience in the last 30 years,

00:06:56.350 --> 00:06:58.870 align:middle line:84%
is that when you talk
about a carbon price,

00:06:58.870 --> 00:07:00.580 align:middle line:90%
it always sounds bad.

00:07:00.580 --> 00:07:02.378 align:middle line:90%
It's immoral.

00:07:02.378 --> 00:07:03.170 align:middle line:90%
Talk to a minister.

00:07:03.170 --> 00:07:06.400 align:middle line:90%


00:07:06.400 --> 00:07:08.350 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, you're right,
but it's immoral.

00:07:08.350 --> 00:07:12.100 align:middle line:84%
And at that point of time,
you say, OK, you prefer

00:07:12.100 --> 00:07:14.620 align:middle line:90%
people polluting for free?

00:07:14.620 --> 00:07:15.680 align:middle line:90%
Is that more moral?

00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:18.450 align:middle line:90%
But I don't know.

00:07:18.450 --> 00:07:21.060 align:middle line:90%
And I think-- we are economists.

00:07:21.060 --> 00:07:24.780 align:middle line:84%
And we have to realize
that, in civil society,

00:07:24.780 --> 00:07:27.480 align:middle line:90%
our ideas are kind of strange--

00:07:27.480 --> 00:07:30.750 align:middle line:84%
views are strange
to some extent.

00:07:30.750 --> 00:07:33.720 align:middle line:84%
And the ideas are really
about market incentive.

00:07:33.720 --> 00:07:37.550 align:middle line:84%
It's true that much of the work
we have done in the last 30

00:07:37.550 --> 00:07:40.170 align:middle line:84%
or 40 years are
about, basically,

00:07:40.170 --> 00:07:41.820 align:middle line:84%
when those markets
don't work and when

00:07:41.820 --> 00:07:44.280 align:middle line:90%
the incentives don't work.

00:07:44.280 --> 00:07:48.900 align:middle line:84%
So there is a huge number
of papers on why low-powered

00:07:48.900 --> 00:07:53.100 align:middle line:84%
incentive schemes might be
desirable because you have noisy

00:07:53.100 --> 00:07:57.270 align:middle line:84%
performance measurement; team
work, which is a special case--

00:07:57.270 --> 00:07:59.520 align:middle line:90%
I think about banks' paper--

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:02.520 align:middle line:84%
collusion with monitors
might work with--

00:08:02.520 --> 00:08:07.980 align:middle line:84%
might work on capture; repeated
interactions; multitasking;

00:08:07.980 --> 00:08:11.170 align:middle line:84%
asymmetric information;
and so on and so forth.

00:08:11.170 --> 00:08:15.000 align:middle line:84%
So there is a huge number
of pieces of theory

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:18.250 align:middle line:84%
which basically says,
low-powered incentive scheme

00:08:18.250 --> 00:08:22.430 align:middle line:84%
can be good sometimes, in
specific circumstances.

00:08:22.430 --> 00:08:23.200 align:middle line:90%
And same thing.

00:08:23.200 --> 00:08:27.100 align:middle line:84%
There are a number of papers
about the fact that incentives

00:08:27.100 --> 00:08:30.655 align:middle line:84%
can crowd out
intrinsic motivation.

00:08:30.655 --> 00:08:34.760 align:middle line:84%
So the two papers with
Bénabou are along those lines.

00:08:34.760 --> 00:08:37.960 align:middle line:84%
The first line is basically
that, if I give you

00:08:37.960 --> 00:08:41.710 align:middle line:84%
an incentive, I may reveal
something about your-- the trust

00:08:41.710 --> 00:08:43.730 align:middle line:84%
they have in you
or about the task.

00:08:43.730 --> 00:08:47.740 align:middle line:84%
And that may actually backfire
because it conveys information

00:08:47.740 --> 00:08:50.020 align:middle line:90%
to you that discourages you.

00:08:50.020 --> 00:08:54.430 align:middle line:84%
And the second one, which is
more familiar from psychology,

00:08:54.430 --> 00:08:57.070 align:middle line:90%
is the idea of--

00:08:57.070 --> 00:09:00.640 align:middle line:84%
that if I pay you
to do something,

00:09:00.640 --> 00:09:04.288 align:middle line:84%
like giving your
blood, then people

00:09:04.288 --> 00:09:06.580 align:middle line:84%
don't know whether you're
giving your blood because you

00:09:06.580 --> 00:09:10.270 align:middle line:84%
are generous or because you are
greedy and you want the money.

00:09:10.270 --> 00:09:11.390 align:middle line:90%
So that kind of stuff.

00:09:11.390 --> 00:09:16.560 align:middle line:84%
So those are the two papers
I had with Roland Bénabou.

00:09:16.560 --> 00:09:18.110 align:middle line:90%
But I could go on and on.

00:09:18.110 --> 00:09:21.610 align:middle line:84%
So much of our work is
actually about cases

00:09:21.610 --> 00:09:25.700 align:middle line:84%
in which markets don't work,
in which incentives don't work.

00:09:25.700 --> 00:09:31.120 align:middle line:84%
But nonetheless, it is
the case that we are still

00:09:31.120 --> 00:09:33.040 align:middle line:84%
more pro-market
and pro-incentive

00:09:33.040 --> 00:09:35.450 align:middle line:84%
than the average person
in the population.

00:09:35.450 --> 00:09:38.730 align:middle line:84%
And the same thing for
the empirical work.

00:09:38.730 --> 00:09:41.930 align:middle line:84%
There's a lot of empirical work
showing that actually incentives

00:09:41.930 --> 00:09:43.850 align:middle line:90%
may be needed.

00:09:43.850 --> 00:09:49.940 align:middle line:84%
And that's something that
is not very well received.

00:09:49.940 --> 00:09:51.300 align:middle line:90%
You can ask why.

00:09:51.300 --> 00:09:52.450 align:middle line:90%
Why is that the case?

00:09:52.450 --> 00:09:55.640 align:middle line:84%
I mean, part of the
thing is that we

00:09:55.640 --> 00:09:58.250 align:middle line:90%
are the bearer of bad news.

00:09:58.250 --> 00:10:01.760 align:middle line:84%
Because when we say
we need incentives,

00:10:01.760 --> 00:10:04.880 align:middle line:84%
we implicitly say
people are not as nice

00:10:04.880 --> 00:10:07.120 align:middle line:90%
as we would like them to be.

00:10:07.120 --> 00:10:10.750 align:middle line:84%
That's exactly the
point, which is,

00:10:10.750 --> 00:10:15.020 align:middle line:84%
we would like people to be nice,
but they are not that nice.

00:10:15.020 --> 00:10:17.595 align:middle line:84%
They keep on polluting,
et cetera, et cetera.

00:10:17.595 --> 00:10:20.310 align:middle line:90%


00:10:20.310 --> 00:10:23.220 align:middle line:90%
And that triggers a reaction.

00:10:23.220 --> 00:10:26.270 align:middle line:84%
In France, every time you
talk about the carbon price,

00:10:26.270 --> 00:10:28.890 align:middle line:84%
people say, the
environment should not

00:10:28.890 --> 00:10:32.340 align:middle line:84%
be-- protecting the environment
should not be punitive.

00:10:32.340 --> 00:10:35.340 align:middle line:90%
It's always the same thing.

00:10:35.340 --> 00:10:37.210 align:middle line:90%
You don't trust people.

00:10:37.210 --> 00:10:37.870 align:middle line:90%
I mean, sure.

00:10:37.870 --> 00:10:39.460 align:middle line:90%
I mean, I don't trust people.

00:10:39.460 --> 00:10:41.640 align:middle line:84%
I mean, for over
the last 30 years,

00:10:41.640 --> 00:10:44.380 align:middle line:84%
nothing has happened in the
fight against climate change.

00:10:44.380 --> 00:10:45.110 align:middle line:90%
Come on.

00:10:45.110 --> 00:10:47.910 align:middle line:84%
But still, they still
say, oh, no, no.

00:10:47.910 --> 00:10:49.750 align:middle line:90%
People will do what it takes.

00:10:49.750 --> 00:10:50.620 align:middle line:90%
And come on.

00:10:50.620 --> 00:10:54.720 align:middle line:84%
I mean, the answer is, no,
it's not going to happen.

00:10:54.720 --> 00:10:57.600 align:middle line:90%
The planet is going to burn.

00:10:57.600 --> 00:10:59.110 align:middle line:90%
So I don't know.

00:10:59.110 --> 00:11:02.070 align:middle line:90%
I mean, it's just like--

00:11:02.070 --> 00:11:05.640 align:middle line:84%
But so, still, despite
all the caveats

00:11:05.640 --> 00:11:08.310 align:middle line:84%
we have been working
on for decades,

00:11:08.310 --> 00:11:11.775 align:middle line:84%
it's still the case that we are
pro-market and pro-incentive

00:11:11.775 --> 00:11:15.080 align:middle line:84%
on the whole, compared
to the population.

00:11:15.080 --> 00:11:18.450 align:middle line:90%


00:11:18.450 --> 00:11:20.730 align:middle line:90%
And then the psychologists--

00:11:20.730 --> 00:11:24.810 align:middle line:84%
I already talked
about those ones.

00:11:24.810 --> 00:11:28.980 align:middle line:84%
But the legal scholars actually
have another line of attack,

00:11:28.980 --> 00:11:30.450 align:middle line:90%
which is interesting.

00:11:30.450 --> 00:11:34.100 align:middle line:84%
It's about the expressive
content of the law.

00:11:34.100 --> 00:11:36.600 align:middle line:84%
So basically, the idea
is the law is not just

00:11:36.600 --> 00:11:37.480 align:middle line:90%
an incentive scheme.

00:11:37.480 --> 00:11:40.230 align:middle line:84%
I mean, we tend to see the
law as an incentive scheme.

00:11:40.230 --> 00:11:42.280 align:middle line:84%
You have to pay a fine
or you go to jail.

00:11:42.280 --> 00:11:47.220 align:middle line:84%
I mean, that's going to
incentivize you to behave well.

00:11:47.220 --> 00:11:49.170 align:middle line:84%
Legal scholars,
they go beyond that.

00:11:49.170 --> 00:11:53.520 align:middle line:84%
They say, no, but the law
also sends a signal to society

00:11:53.520 --> 00:11:56.550 align:middle line:84%
about the norm in society,
about the views, what

00:11:56.550 --> 00:11:58.390 align:middle line:90%
is acceptable or not.

00:11:58.390 --> 00:12:01.965 align:middle line:90%
So it goes beyond that.

00:12:01.965 --> 00:12:05.520 align:middle line:84%
And then, philosophers
I mentioned [INAUDIBLE].

00:12:05.520 --> 00:12:11.450 align:middle line:84%
So they are unhappy
with markets.

00:12:11.450 --> 00:12:14.160 align:middle line:84%
Now, I'm not going
to go into that.

00:12:14.160 --> 00:12:16.460 align:middle line:84%
But in Economics
for the Common Good,

00:12:16.460 --> 00:12:17.960 align:middle line:84%
I spent a fair
amount of time trying

00:12:17.960 --> 00:12:22.190 align:middle line:84%
to explain that a number
of repugnant markets

00:12:22.190 --> 00:12:26.630 align:middle line:84%
are actually markets
with externalities,

00:12:26.630 --> 00:12:28.610 align:middle line:84%
with internalities,
with whatever,

00:12:28.610 --> 00:12:30.680 align:middle line:90%
asymmetric information.

00:12:30.680 --> 00:12:33.920 align:middle line:84%
They can be explained in
the framework of economics.

00:12:33.920 --> 00:12:36.020 align:middle line:84%
There is no contradiction
between economics

00:12:36.020 --> 00:12:37.447 align:middle line:90%
and the repugnant market.

00:12:37.447 --> 00:12:39.030 align:middle line:84%
I mean, just take
babies for adoption.

00:12:39.030 --> 00:12:43.100 align:middle line:84%
I mean, we will not want
the babies for adoption

00:12:43.100 --> 00:12:46.790 align:middle line:84%
to be sold to the
highest bidder.

00:12:46.790 --> 00:12:49.470 align:middle line:84%
Well, I mean, you might
say, well, after all,

00:12:49.470 --> 00:12:51.470 align:middle line:84%
if someone is willing to
pay a lot for the baby,

00:12:51.470 --> 00:12:54.660 align:middle line:84%
then maybe that person is going
to benefit more for the baby.

00:12:54.660 --> 00:12:58.160 align:middle line:84%
But there is a third
party who is not

00:12:58.160 --> 00:12:59.370 align:middle line:90%
part of the contract there.

00:12:59.370 --> 00:13:01.100 align:middle line:90%
It's the baby.

00:13:01.100 --> 00:13:02.930 align:middle line:84%
You have a huge
externality on the baby.

00:13:02.930 --> 00:13:06.050 align:middle line:84%
You have no guarantee that
actually the highest bidder

00:13:06.050 --> 00:13:10.935 align:middle line:84%
is going to be the
best father or mother.

00:13:10.935 --> 00:13:12.710 align:middle line:90%
But it goes beyond that.

00:13:12.710 --> 00:13:15.050 align:middle line:84%
Also, something which is
interesting, which is image

00:13:15.050 --> 00:13:16.310 align:middle line:90%
externalities.

00:13:16.310 --> 00:13:18.080 align:middle line:90%
So that I discuss.

00:13:18.080 --> 00:13:22.310 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes, you could have
two parties who are actually

00:13:22.310 --> 00:13:25.610 align:middle line:84%
part of a contract, but
their contract actually

00:13:25.610 --> 00:13:29.280 align:middle line:84%
may shed bad light on the
rest of the population.

00:13:29.280 --> 00:13:32.270 align:middle line:84%
And image externalities
is the thing.

00:13:32.270 --> 00:13:34.280 align:middle line:84%
Then there are all
kinds of internalities,

00:13:34.280 --> 00:13:39.320 align:middle line:84%
but internalities like
present bias, for example,

00:13:39.320 --> 00:13:41.880 align:middle line:84%
is just an externality on
yourself, on your future self.

00:13:41.880 --> 00:13:43.280 align:middle line:90%
So it's the same thing.

00:13:43.280 --> 00:13:52.240 align:middle line:84%
So we don't like people
who sell drugs or opioids

00:13:52.240 --> 00:13:54.460 align:middle line:90%
or things like that.

00:13:54.460 --> 00:13:57.790 align:middle line:84%
And of course, we are
against involuntary slavery

00:13:57.790 --> 00:14:00.400 align:middle line:84%
because that's a
huge externality.

00:14:00.400 --> 00:14:05.140 align:middle line:84%
But we are even against
voluntary slavery.

00:14:05.140 --> 00:14:07.390 align:middle line:84%
And you might say, why
not voluntary slavery?

00:14:07.390 --> 00:14:10.870 align:middle line:84%
If everybody agrees,
why not, to a contract?

00:14:10.870 --> 00:14:15.250 align:middle line:84%
But of course, there
are present biases which

00:14:15.250 --> 00:14:18.190 align:middle line:90%
could be growing those markets.

00:14:18.190 --> 00:14:20.260 align:middle line:90%
Asymmetric information.

00:14:20.260 --> 00:14:22.870 align:middle line:84%
And [? Sander, ?] for
example, says, how?

00:14:22.870 --> 00:14:26.620 align:middle line:84%
Those economists will say,
OK, just pay to get friends.

00:14:26.620 --> 00:14:29.050 align:middle line:84%
I mean, but if you
pay to get a friend,

00:14:29.050 --> 00:14:31.160 align:middle line:84%
then how do you know this
person is your friend?

00:14:31.160 --> 00:14:34.190 align:middle line:84%
I mean, it's called
asymmetric information.

00:14:34.190 --> 00:14:36.014 align:middle line:90%
It makes no sense.

00:14:36.014 --> 00:14:40.960 align:middle line:84%
So there is at least a number
of those things that can be.

00:14:40.960 --> 00:14:45.880 align:middle line:84%
It's just a consequence
of standard economics.

00:14:45.880 --> 00:14:52.070 align:middle line:84%
But the thing I want to
insist on is moral postures.

00:14:52.070 --> 00:14:53.540 align:middle line:90%
I don't like moral postures.

00:14:53.540 --> 00:14:56.380 align:middle line:84%
I mean, that's my
own bias, which

00:14:56.380 --> 00:14:59.840 align:middle line:84%
is they can be useful
as a warning signal.

00:14:59.840 --> 00:15:04.150 align:middle line:84%
So I see something,
I get some emotion,

00:15:04.150 --> 00:15:07.630 align:middle line:84%
and that might suggest that
something might be wrong.

00:15:07.630 --> 00:15:09.650 align:middle line:90%
So it's a warning signal.

00:15:09.650 --> 00:15:10.850 align:middle line:90%
It actually can be useful.

00:15:10.850 --> 00:15:14.980 align:middle line:84%
But I don't like people who
just stay with the emotion

00:15:14.980 --> 00:15:19.290 align:middle line:84%
and say, I don't like
it and this is immoral,

00:15:19.290 --> 00:15:20.940 align:middle line:90%
without explaining why.

00:15:20.940 --> 00:15:27.030 align:middle line:84%
And that's something-- because
if you say, in the old time,

00:15:27.030 --> 00:15:30.670 align:middle line:84%
for example, life
insurance was immoral.

00:15:30.670 --> 00:15:33.120 align:middle line:84%
Everybody thought life
insurance was immoral.

00:15:33.120 --> 00:15:34.010 align:middle line:90%
Now, why?

00:15:34.010 --> 00:15:37.450 align:middle line:84%
I mean, so they would say, oh,
yeah, it has to do with life.

00:15:37.450 --> 00:15:38.060 align:middle line:90%
Sure.

00:15:38.060 --> 00:15:40.790 align:middle line:90%
Come on.

00:15:40.790 --> 00:15:45.230 align:middle line:84%
Same thing in many countries
today and in most countries

00:15:45.230 --> 00:15:49.280 align:middle line:84%
yesterday, having sex among
people of the same sex

00:15:49.280 --> 00:15:53.030 align:middle line:84%
or different races, for example,
was thought of as "immoral"

00:15:53.030 --> 00:15:54.800 align:middle line:90%
by a majority of people.

00:15:54.800 --> 00:15:57.530 align:middle line:84%
And that was just a way
of actually infringing

00:15:57.530 --> 00:15:59.110 align:middle line:90%
on the freedom of people.

00:15:59.110 --> 00:16:04.160 align:middle line:84%
So those moral postures,
I'm always very nervous.

00:16:04.160 --> 00:16:07.430 align:middle line:84%
And when I hear
philosophers telling me,

00:16:07.430 --> 00:16:11.186 align:middle line:84%
oh, this is immoral,
period, I just--

00:16:11.186 --> 00:16:13.820 align:middle line:90%
I just don't like it.

00:16:13.820 --> 00:16:16.800 align:middle line:84%
Again, the warning
signal is pretty fine.

00:16:16.800 --> 00:16:20.160 align:middle line:84%
And our emotions actually
can reveal real issues.

00:16:20.160 --> 00:16:23.630 align:middle line:84%
But you have to go
the next step and say,

00:16:23.630 --> 00:16:25.760 align:middle line:84%
let's think a little
bit more about this.

00:16:25.760 --> 00:16:29.400 align:middle line:84%
Is there an externality,
is someone hurt,

00:16:29.400 --> 00:16:31.382 align:middle line:90%
and so on and so forth.

00:16:31.382 --> 00:16:34.230 align:middle line:84%
So that's just my
own view on this.

00:16:34.230 --> 00:16:34.800 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:16:34.800 --> 00:16:36.120 align:middle line:90%
Questions?

00:16:36.120 --> 00:16:37.650 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:16:37.650 --> 00:16:41.970 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: How could we differ
or think about the difference

00:16:41.970 --> 00:16:47.760 align:middle line:84%
from someone having moral
cost of taking some action

00:16:47.760 --> 00:16:53.700 align:middle line:84%
or consuming some thing and then
someone having blockage, where

00:16:53.700 --> 00:16:56.220 align:middle line:84%
there's a cutoff
after which they just

00:16:56.220 --> 00:16:59.810 align:middle line:90%
stop even considering that--

00:16:59.810 --> 00:17:01.520 align:middle line:84%
considerations that
it's a possible thing

00:17:01.520 --> 00:17:03.470 align:middle line:90%
that we could do, for instance?

00:17:03.470 --> 00:17:04.970 align:middle line:84%
JEAN TIROLE: So
there's this saying

00:17:04.970 --> 00:17:11.710 align:middle line:84%
that the very act of
thinking about something

00:17:11.710 --> 00:17:14.960 align:middle line:90%
actually reveals already stuff.

00:17:14.960 --> 00:17:15.845 align:middle line:90%
There is this movie.

00:17:15.845 --> 00:17:16.970 align:middle line:90%
I don't remember the title.

00:17:16.970 --> 00:17:18.290 align:middle line:90%
It was not such a good movie.

00:17:18.290 --> 00:17:22.369 align:middle line:84%
But there was this movie
in which a millionaire

00:17:22.369 --> 00:17:28.610 align:middle line:84%
was offering-- there was a
young couple, very good, united

00:17:28.610 --> 00:17:29.570 align:middle line:90%
couple.

00:17:29.570 --> 00:17:31.520 align:middle line:90%
And then the millionaire comes.

00:17:31.520 --> 00:17:33.350 align:middle line:90%
But they are poor.

00:17:33.350 --> 00:17:36.350 align:middle line:84%
And the millionaire comes
in and say, I pay $1 million

00:17:36.350 --> 00:17:38.210 align:middle line:90%
to sleep with your wife.

00:17:38.210 --> 00:17:42.310 align:middle line:84%
And then they start
entertaining the thought.

00:17:42.310 --> 00:17:44.600 align:middle line:84%
And I actually don't remember
what happens in the end.

00:17:44.600 --> 00:17:47.740 align:middle line:84%
[CHUCKLES] But they start
entertaining the thought,

00:17:47.740 --> 00:17:49.060 align:middle line:90%
and that destroys her.

00:17:49.060 --> 00:17:53.960 align:middle line:84%
Even entertaining the
thought destroys that couple.

00:17:53.960 --> 00:17:57.340 align:middle line:84%
Not doing it, just
thinking about it.

00:17:57.340 --> 00:17:58.330 align:middle line:90%
And same thing.

00:17:58.330 --> 00:18:04.060 align:middle line:84%
If you tell a Catholic you have
to think about whether Mary

00:18:04.060 --> 00:18:09.970 align:middle line:84%
could be a virgin or whatever,
just starting to question things

00:18:09.970 --> 00:18:11.350 align:middle line:90%
may actually--

00:18:11.350 --> 00:18:12.520 align:middle line:90%
already the signal.

00:18:12.520 --> 00:18:13.935 align:middle line:84%
Is that what you
had in mind or--

00:18:13.935 --> 00:18:14.560 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:18:14.560 --> 00:18:15.310 align:middle line:90%
JEAN TIROLE: Yeah.

00:18:15.310 --> 00:18:17.856 align:middle line:90%


00:18:17.856 --> 00:18:23.250 align:middle line:84%
And sometimes, we economists
actually are guilty of that

00:18:23.250 --> 00:18:25.440 align:middle line:90%
in a sense.

00:18:25.440 --> 00:18:30.730 align:middle line:84%
You may remember Larry
Summers talking about taking--

00:18:30.730 --> 00:18:33.540 align:middle line:90%


00:18:33.540 --> 00:18:36.810 align:middle line:84%
polluting stuff to
less-developed countries

00:18:36.810 --> 00:18:40.470 align:middle line:84%
because they are willing
to get less money in order

00:18:40.470 --> 00:18:42.240 align:middle line:90%
to take care of the stuff.

00:18:42.240 --> 00:18:44.350 align:middle line:90%
And that was a big scandal.

00:18:44.350 --> 00:18:46.680 align:middle line:84%
I don't remember if
he was recommending

00:18:46.680 --> 00:18:48.930 align:middle line:90%
it, which is a different thing.

00:18:48.930 --> 00:18:51.645 align:middle line:84%
Just thinking about it
was just already thought

00:18:51.645 --> 00:18:56.790 align:middle line:90%
of as being very strange.

00:18:56.790 --> 00:19:01.350 align:middle line:84%
And we often-- actually,
when we do research,

00:19:01.350 --> 00:19:04.440 align:middle line:84%
we often think about
various other things which

00:19:04.440 --> 00:19:08.490 align:middle line:90%
might be thought as not right.

00:19:08.490 --> 00:19:11.620 align:middle line:84%
And I will say, it's part of
the freedom of the researcher.

00:19:11.620 --> 00:19:17.790 align:middle line:84%
Now, when we go public,
it's a different ball game.

00:19:17.790 --> 00:19:21.730 align:middle line:84%
So I would try to
distinguish between my life

00:19:21.730 --> 00:19:24.310 align:middle line:84%
as a researcher,
where I want to enjoy

00:19:24.310 --> 00:19:29.710 align:middle line:84%
maximum freedom of thought, and
then maybe when I go public,

00:19:29.710 --> 00:19:32.360 align:middle line:84%
I may offend some
people and so on.

00:19:32.360 --> 00:19:36.230 align:middle line:90%
And that's a different thing.

00:19:36.230 --> 00:19:39.220 align:middle line:84%
But, of course, there's always
a thin line between the two.

00:19:39.220 --> 00:19:41.880 align:middle line:90%


00:19:41.880 --> 00:19:45.300 align:middle line:84%
Now, if you think about how
our societies are organized--

00:19:45.300 --> 00:19:47.250 align:middle line:84%
and you know all
of that, but I just

00:19:47.250 --> 00:19:49.926 align:middle line:90%
want to give you the framework.

00:19:49.926 --> 00:19:52.490 align:middle line:90%


00:19:52.490 --> 00:19:57.131 align:middle line:84%
I will say, it's Adam
Smith plus Pigou.

00:19:57.131 --> 00:19:58.810 align:middle line:90%
It's Adam Smith plus Pigou.

00:19:58.810 --> 00:20:06.120 align:middle line:84%
So the very bizarre thing
is that, by and large, we

00:20:06.120 --> 00:20:09.970 align:middle line:90%
have for-profit corporations.

00:20:09.970 --> 00:20:15.220 align:middle line:84%
So for-profit
corporations are entities

00:20:15.220 --> 00:20:20.552 align:middle line:84%
in which the decision-making
obeys the shareholders.

00:20:20.552 --> 00:20:23.690 align:middle line:90%
The shareholders decide.

00:20:23.690 --> 00:20:25.920 align:middle line:90%
Of course, it's not quite true.

00:20:25.920 --> 00:20:28.620 align:middle line:84%
You have agency issues
with the managers.

00:20:28.620 --> 00:20:30.470 align:middle line:84%
I mean, we have worked
on all those things.

00:20:30.470 --> 00:20:33.880 align:middle line:84%
But basically, it's the
shareholders who decide.

00:20:33.880 --> 00:20:37.600 align:middle line:84%
Now, we know why it's
the case, because that's

00:20:37.600 --> 00:20:41.530 align:middle line:84%
where the shareholders make
sure they get their money back.

00:20:41.530 --> 00:20:44.650 align:middle line:84%
Otherwise, they might not get
their money back, or investors,

00:20:44.650 --> 00:20:45.430 align:middle line:90%
more generally.

00:20:45.430 --> 00:20:49.240 align:middle line:84%
But it still raises the issue
that shareholders, of course,

00:20:49.240 --> 00:20:50.810 align:middle line:90%
are interested in the decision.

00:20:50.810 --> 00:20:52.450 align:middle line:90%
They want their money back.

00:20:52.450 --> 00:20:56.740 align:middle line:84%
But what about workers and
suppliers and customers

00:20:56.740 --> 00:20:57.280 align:middle line:90%
and so on?

00:20:57.280 --> 00:21:02.980 align:middle line:84%
There are many stakeholders
who are actually left--

00:21:02.980 --> 00:21:06.730 align:middle line:84%
left aside in the
decision prime, right?

00:21:06.730 --> 00:21:10.600 align:middle line:84%
So we have this situation
where one of the stakeholders

00:21:10.600 --> 00:21:11.930 align:middle line:90%
takes a decision.

00:21:11.930 --> 00:21:13.540 align:middle line:90%
Those are the investors.

00:21:13.540 --> 00:21:16.960 align:middle line:84%
And the others are
basically left--

00:21:16.960 --> 00:21:20.040 align:middle line:90%
they are decision externalities.

00:21:20.040 --> 00:21:25.320 align:middle line:84%
So that speaks to the corporate
social responsibility.

00:21:25.320 --> 00:21:28.680 align:middle line:90%


00:21:28.680 --> 00:21:32.910 align:middle line:84%
Friedman had this famous
column in which he said,

00:21:32.910 --> 00:21:36.390 align:middle line:84%
the responsibility of business
is actually to maximize profit,

00:21:36.390 --> 00:21:40.980 align:middle line:84%
not to take into account
the stakeholders' welfare.

00:21:40.980 --> 00:21:45.060 align:middle line:84%
And his colleague at
Chicago actually said, well,

00:21:45.060 --> 00:21:46.950 align:middle line:84%
if you want to
protect shareholders--

00:21:46.950 --> 00:21:50.490 align:middle line:90%
this is Ronald Coase--

00:21:50.490 --> 00:21:52.620 align:middle line:84%
the first one was
Milton Friedman.

00:21:52.620 --> 00:21:53.250 align:middle line:90%
Sorry.

00:21:53.250 --> 00:21:55.050 align:middle line:84%
I realize your
generation may never

00:21:55.050 --> 00:21:58.890 align:middle line:84%
have seen a picture of Milton
Friedman or Ronald Coase.

00:21:58.890 --> 00:22:02.340 align:middle line:84%
So Coase basically said,
well, let's have contracts

00:22:02.340 --> 00:22:03.520 align:middle line:90%
to protect shareholders.

00:22:03.520 --> 00:22:06.270 align:middle line:84%
And to some extent,
that's being done.

00:22:06.270 --> 00:22:12.870 align:middle line:84%
So the contracts which insulate
the most the workers or the debt

00:22:12.870 --> 00:22:18.660 align:middle line:84%
holders and so on against the
decisions of shareholders are--

00:22:18.660 --> 00:22:21.360 align:middle line:84%
say, for example, the
worker is a fixed wage

00:22:21.360 --> 00:22:25.880 align:middle line:84%
plus some severance pay
if they are laid off.

00:22:25.880 --> 00:22:29.930 align:middle line:84%
Same thing if you look at
debt, although they get often

00:22:29.930 --> 00:22:36.710 align:middle line:84%
some fixed debt repayment plus
some collateral pledging in case

00:22:36.710 --> 00:22:39.170 align:middle line:90%
the firm goes bankrupt.

00:22:39.170 --> 00:22:43.535 align:middle line:84%
And you could also have
exit options and the like.

00:22:43.535 --> 00:22:47.390 align:middle line:84%
So basically the idea,
OK, shareholders decide--

00:22:47.390 --> 00:22:49.400 align:middle line:90%
that's Ronald Coase in a sense--

00:22:49.400 --> 00:22:52.160 align:middle line:84%
shareholders decide, but
you protect the stakeholders

00:22:52.160 --> 00:22:54.320 align:middle line:90%
through contracting.

00:22:54.320 --> 00:22:57.570 align:middle line:84%
The next step is,
oh, yeah, but what

00:22:57.570 --> 00:23:01.530 align:middle line:84%
about the quality of
contracting, of course?

00:23:01.530 --> 00:23:05.250 align:middle line:84%
Imperfect contracts,
collective action prime,

00:23:05.250 --> 00:23:07.590 align:middle line:84%
and the environment
and competition policy

00:23:07.590 --> 00:23:08.830 align:middle line:90%
are a good example.

00:23:08.830 --> 00:23:11.100 align:middle line:84%
You have millions of
people who are affected

00:23:11.100 --> 00:23:13.200 align:middle line:84%
by the decision of the
firm, and, of course,

00:23:13.200 --> 00:23:15.450 align:middle line:84%
it's very hard for them
to contract with the firms

00:23:15.450 --> 00:23:17.070 align:middle line:90%
and so on.

00:23:17.070 --> 00:23:20.380 align:middle line:84%
And there are many
other issues with Coase.

00:23:20.380 --> 00:23:22.950 align:middle line:84%
So in a sense, what
we have converged

00:23:22.950 --> 00:23:26.370 align:middle line:90%
to is some kind of mixture of--

00:23:26.370 --> 00:23:29.760 align:middle line:84%
you know which one was
Smith and was Pigou, right--

00:23:29.760 --> 00:23:34.330 align:middle line:90%


00:23:34.330 --> 00:23:40.560 align:middle line:84%
to mix basically a market, the
invisible hand of the market

00:23:40.560 --> 00:23:44.200 align:middle line:90%
together with regulation.

00:23:44.200 --> 00:23:46.290 align:middle line:90%
So in a sense--

00:23:46.290 --> 00:23:48.720 align:middle line:84%
the other way you can
protect stakeholders

00:23:48.720 --> 00:23:50.910 align:middle line:90%
is to have a regulation.

00:23:50.910 --> 00:23:52.830 align:middle line:84%
You have an
environmental agency.

00:23:52.830 --> 00:23:55.140 align:middle line:90%
You have a competition policy.

00:23:55.140 --> 00:23:56.920 align:middle line:90%
You have a banking regulator.

00:23:56.920 --> 00:23:58.770 align:middle line:84%
You have someone
in government who

00:23:58.770 --> 00:24:02.475 align:middle line:84%
is going to protect
the stakeholders.

00:24:02.475 --> 00:24:04.210 align:middle line:90%
So that sort of thing.

00:24:04.210 --> 00:24:07.430 align:middle line:90%


00:24:07.430 --> 00:24:12.620 align:middle line:84%
Now, in this world, where
you have the market which

00:24:12.620 --> 00:24:16.130 align:middle line:84%
is pretty efficient but has
lots of market failures,

00:24:16.130 --> 00:24:18.360 align:middle line:84%
then the government
corrects a market failure.

00:24:18.360 --> 00:24:21.290 align:middle line:90%
It's a simple world.

00:24:21.290 --> 00:24:25.250 align:middle line:84%
We are "market
fixers" in a sense.

00:24:25.250 --> 00:24:29.270 align:middle line:84%
Why should there be any
corporate social responsibility

00:24:29.270 --> 00:24:34.610 align:middle line:84%
or social responsibility
as an investor?

00:24:34.610 --> 00:24:37.340 align:middle line:90%
And the answer is there is none.

00:24:37.340 --> 00:24:39.290 align:middle line:84%
If the government
does their job,

00:24:39.290 --> 00:24:41.660 align:middle line:90%
you don't have to do their job.

00:24:41.660 --> 00:24:43.940 align:middle line:84%
The problem is that,
in practice, there

00:24:43.940 --> 00:24:47.760 align:middle line:90%
is a twin failure between--

00:24:47.760 --> 00:24:50.950 align:middle line:84%
so there is a market failure
plus a government failure.

00:24:50.950 --> 00:24:57.130 align:middle line:84%
And the carbon case is a typical
example of a market failure.

00:24:57.130 --> 00:25:00.790 align:middle line:84%
The firms and the
individuals can emit carbon.

00:25:00.790 --> 00:25:03.100 align:middle line:90%
They have no incentive not to.

00:25:03.100 --> 00:25:08.280 align:middle line:84%
And the government doesn't want
to have a carbon tax, period.

00:25:08.280 --> 00:25:10.000 align:middle line:90%
So you have a twin failure.

00:25:10.000 --> 00:25:13.110 align:middle line:84%
Now we can ask, why is
that the government fails?

00:25:13.110 --> 00:25:16.050 align:middle line:84%
And there are many
reasons for that.

00:25:16.050 --> 00:25:18.810 align:middle line:84%
So then, given that
the market fails,

00:25:18.810 --> 00:25:21.180 align:middle line:84%
the government
fails, what is left?

00:25:21.180 --> 00:25:22.630 align:middle line:90%
Civil society.

00:25:22.630 --> 00:25:26.550 align:middle line:84%
And that's why we have
a social responsibility.

00:25:26.550 --> 00:25:29.550 align:middle line:84%
I told you a little bit about
the repugnant market, which

00:25:29.550 --> 00:25:32.310 align:middle line:84%
is only a subset of
markets which are attacked.

00:25:32.310 --> 00:25:35.340 align:middle line:84%
But there is a broader
condemnation of markets--

00:25:35.340 --> 00:25:39.060 align:middle line:90%
in a sense, all markets--

00:25:39.060 --> 00:25:42.300 align:middle line:84%
on the basis that the
social value of activities

00:25:42.300 --> 00:25:44.410 align:middle line:90%
may differ from private gain.

00:25:44.410 --> 00:25:50.400 align:middle line:84%
That's something that economists
worked on all the time.

00:25:50.400 --> 00:25:55.740 align:middle line:84%
In a sense, we know about
externalities and the like.

00:25:55.740 --> 00:25:59.620 align:middle line:84%
And something I'm going
to discuss in the--

00:25:59.620 --> 00:26:02.100 align:middle line:84%
I discuss in the work
with Mathias Dewatripont

00:26:02.100 --> 00:26:04.560 align:middle line:84%
is the idea, which
is more subtle,

00:26:04.560 --> 00:26:09.850 align:middle line:84%
that markets can actually
frame ethical choices.

00:26:09.850 --> 00:26:18.040 align:middle line:84%
So the idea is that the managers
confronted with this twin market

00:26:18.040 --> 00:26:19.630 align:middle line:90%
plus government failure.

00:26:19.630 --> 00:26:21.760 align:middle line:84%
The managers actually
could substitute

00:26:21.760 --> 00:26:23.640 align:middle line:84%
and say, OK, I'm going
to behave morally.

00:26:23.640 --> 00:26:29.530 align:middle line:90%


00:26:29.530 --> 00:26:33.370 align:middle line:84%
Now, there's the issue about
whether they will be allowed

00:26:33.370 --> 00:26:35.530 align:middle line:90%
by shareholders to do that.

00:26:35.530 --> 00:26:37.000 align:middle line:84%
But same thing--
the shareholders

00:26:37.000 --> 00:26:39.790 align:middle line:90%
could behave morally.

00:26:39.790 --> 00:26:41.350 align:middle line:84%
If they are socially
responsible,

00:26:41.350 --> 00:26:42.500 align:middle line:90%
they could behave morally.

00:26:42.500 --> 00:26:46.426 align:middle line:90%


00:26:46.426 --> 00:26:50.170 align:middle line:84%
But still, the market
might be creating issues

00:26:50.170 --> 00:26:54.940 align:middle line:84%
with moral behavior, especially
if you are a consequentialist,

00:26:54.940 --> 00:26:55.950 align:middle line:90%
which I hope you are.

00:26:55.950 --> 00:26:58.800 align:middle line:90%


00:26:58.800 --> 00:27:00.180 align:middle line:90%
I don't know.

00:27:00.180 --> 00:27:02.640 align:middle line:84%
I'm not going to
impose values on you.

00:27:02.640 --> 00:27:03.880 align:middle line:90%
That doesn't make sense.

00:27:03.880 --> 00:27:06.480 align:middle line:84%
But consequentialist
means you care

00:27:06.480 --> 00:27:09.050 align:middle line:90%
about consequences of things.

00:27:09.050 --> 00:27:13.044 align:middle line:90%


00:27:13.044 --> 00:27:18.120 align:middle line:84%
And actually, this is, I think,
the most potent criticism

00:27:18.120 --> 00:27:22.090 align:middle line:90%
of consequentialism.

00:27:22.090 --> 00:27:27.610 align:middle line:84%
So the issue about the
replacement logic is basically,

00:27:27.610 --> 00:27:28.870 align:middle line:90%
I don't like to pollute.

00:27:28.870 --> 00:27:32.310 align:middle line:84%
But if I don't pollute,
somebody else will.

00:27:32.310 --> 00:27:35.740 align:middle line:84%
I don't like to sell
weapons to a dictator.

00:27:35.740 --> 00:27:37.490 align:middle line:90%
That's not nice.

00:27:37.490 --> 00:27:41.010 align:middle line:90%
But what the hell?

00:27:41.010 --> 00:27:44.330 align:middle line:84%
If I don't do it,
somebody will sell weapons

00:27:44.330 --> 00:27:46.000 align:middle line:84%
to that dictator,
which is correct.

00:27:46.000 --> 00:27:48.930 align:middle line:90%


00:27:48.930 --> 00:27:50.550 align:middle line:90%
I don't like to bribe officials.

00:27:50.550 --> 00:27:52.250 align:middle line:90%
It's not very moral.

00:27:52.250 --> 00:27:56.240 align:middle line:84%
But if I don't bribe, I don't
get the contract, and someone

00:27:56.240 --> 00:27:57.720 align:middle line:90%
who bribes is going to get it.

00:27:57.720 --> 00:28:00.000 align:middle line:84%
So in the end, the
outcome is the same.

00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:02.840 align:middle line:84%
So I might as well do it myself
because I'd make money out

00:28:02.840 --> 00:28:04.640 align:middle line:90%
of it.

00:28:04.640 --> 00:28:09.620 align:middle line:84%
The doctors who overprescribe
opioids were saying that,

00:28:09.620 --> 00:28:12.890 align:middle line:84%
saying, I don't like to
overprescribe opioids.

00:28:12.890 --> 00:28:22.880 align:middle line:84%
But if I don't do it, some other
doctor will prescribe opioids.

00:28:22.880 --> 00:28:25.370 align:middle line:84%
And the same thing
happens with sick leave.

00:28:25.370 --> 00:28:27.815 align:middle line:84%
So in France, you
go to any doctor.

00:28:27.815 --> 00:28:31.060 align:middle line:90%


00:28:31.060 --> 00:28:35.950 align:middle line:84%
You want a sick leave for
two weeks, the doctor signs.

00:28:35.950 --> 00:28:38.070 align:middle line:84%
Even so, you're
in perfect health.

00:28:38.070 --> 00:28:42.835 align:middle line:84%
But the story is
that, of course,

00:28:42.835 --> 00:28:46.210 align:middle line:84%
if the doctor doesn't do that,
then the next doctor will do it

00:28:46.210 --> 00:28:48.880 align:middle line:90%
anyway.

00:28:48.880 --> 00:28:50.930 align:middle line:84%
And there are many
other examples.

00:28:50.930 --> 00:28:53.290 align:middle line:90%
I mean, athletes, for example.

00:28:53.290 --> 00:28:56.680 align:middle line:84%
Many cyclists don't like
to take those drugs.

00:28:56.680 --> 00:28:59.210 align:middle line:84%
I mean, a football
player and so on,

00:28:59.210 --> 00:29:00.710 align:middle line:84%
they don't like to
take those drugs.

00:29:00.710 --> 00:29:05.830 align:middle line:84%
But they say, it's the
only way I can actually

00:29:05.830 --> 00:29:09.540 align:middle line:90%
compete and so on.

00:29:09.540 --> 00:29:13.850 align:middle line:84%
So you see the idea--
in all those examples,

00:29:13.850 --> 00:29:16.340 align:middle line:84%
there is a common idea that
competitive pressure is going

00:29:16.340 --> 00:29:19.566 align:middle line:90%
to make it hard to actually--

00:29:19.566 --> 00:29:23.140 align:middle line:90%


00:29:23.140 --> 00:29:24.700 align:middle line:90%
to actually compete.

00:29:24.700 --> 00:29:29.050 align:middle line:84%
So the "conclusion," in quotes,
is that you should relax

00:29:29.050 --> 00:29:30.280 align:middle line:90%
competition.

00:29:30.280 --> 00:29:36.172 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, that has deep
implication for [14.]271.

00:29:36.172 --> 00:29:40.420 align:middle line:84%
So that's something
we're going to discuss.

00:29:40.420 --> 00:29:45.730 align:middle line:84%
Now, I already mentioned that
that might have implications

00:29:45.730 --> 00:29:48.130 align:middle line:84%
for competition policies,
which are being discussed right

00:29:48.130 --> 00:29:52.210 align:middle line:84%
now, the promotion of
open platform ecosystems,

00:29:52.210 --> 00:29:57.670 align:middle line:84%
for competitive procurement,
for trade, and so on.

00:29:57.670 --> 00:29:59.800 align:middle line:84%
And that might also
have-- there's also

00:29:59.800 --> 00:30:05.860 align:middle line:84%
a discussion about whether you
should actually either favor

00:30:05.860 --> 00:30:12.220 align:middle line:84%
or just completely
allocate a market to firms

00:30:12.220 --> 00:30:13.410 align:middle line:90%
which are not for profit.

00:30:13.410 --> 00:30:16.200 align:middle line:90%


00:30:16.200 --> 00:30:17.980 align:middle line:90%
So that kind of thing.

00:30:17.980 --> 00:30:22.830 align:middle line:84%
So should we, for
example, make--

00:30:22.830 --> 00:30:24.960 align:middle line:84%
in France, there
was a scandal a year

00:30:24.960 --> 00:30:29.390 align:middle line:90%
ago about a retirement home.

00:30:29.390 --> 00:30:33.440 align:middle line:84%
They charged a lot of money and
provided a very low quality,

00:30:33.440 --> 00:30:36.440 align:middle line:90%
at least some of them.

00:30:36.440 --> 00:30:38.940 align:middle line:84%
Same thing has happened
with hospitals and so on.

00:30:38.940 --> 00:30:41.400 align:middle line:84%
I mean, should they
all be not for profit?

00:30:41.400 --> 00:30:44.580 align:middle line:84%
Should universities
be not for profit?

00:30:44.580 --> 00:30:45.835 align:middle line:90%
Actually, most of them are.

00:30:45.835 --> 00:30:48.790 align:middle line:90%


00:30:48.790 --> 00:30:53.120 align:middle line:84%
Well, they are not
not-for-profit for everyone.

00:30:53.120 --> 00:30:57.740 align:middle line:90%


00:30:57.740 --> 00:31:00.000 align:middle line:84%
Should they be
subsidized and so on?

00:31:00.000 --> 00:31:05.120 align:middle line:90%
So those are important issues.

00:31:05.120 --> 00:31:07.910 align:middle line:84%
I mean, in the last
decades, nobody

00:31:07.910 --> 00:31:11.516 align:middle line:84%
was talking about that that
much, but now it's resurfacing.

00:31:11.516 --> 00:31:14.070 align:middle line:90%


00:31:14.070 --> 00:31:20.000 align:middle line:84%
Just a last caveat before I
go into more technical stuff.

00:31:20.000 --> 00:31:24.300 align:middle line:90%


00:31:24.300 --> 00:31:27.680 align:middle line:84%
I'm not going to invent
anything on morality.

00:31:27.680 --> 00:31:30.600 align:middle line:84%
I mean, people have been
discussing this for millennia.

00:31:30.600 --> 00:31:34.920 align:middle line:84%
So I'm not going to say anything
interesting on morality,

00:31:34.920 --> 00:31:38.760 align:middle line:84%
but I just want to
say, we economists have

00:31:38.760 --> 00:31:42.870 align:middle line:84%
a view of morality
which tends to be,

00:31:42.870 --> 00:31:45.390 align:middle line:84%
in a sense, no
externality or cutoff.

00:31:45.390 --> 00:31:48.360 align:middle line:90%


00:31:48.360 --> 00:31:53.520 align:middle line:84%
Basically, if I stab you, then
you'll find it's not very nice.

00:31:53.520 --> 00:31:56.460 align:middle line:90%
It's not quite moral.

00:31:56.460 --> 00:31:58.505 align:middle line:90%
The avoidance of harm.

00:31:58.505 --> 00:32:01.528 align:middle line:90%


00:32:01.528 --> 00:32:04.340 align:middle line:84%
And I would say that this
kind of conception of morality

00:32:04.340 --> 00:32:10.670 align:middle line:84%
is more stable across
culture and across time,

00:32:10.670 --> 00:32:13.430 align:middle line:90%
but it's restrictive.

00:32:13.430 --> 00:32:16.530 align:middle line:84%
Many people have a much
broader view of morality.

00:32:16.530 --> 00:32:21.380 align:middle line:84%
So if you look at Jonathan
Haidt's books, for example,

00:32:21.380 --> 00:32:27.590 align:middle line:84%
you get lots of examples
of immoral behavior

00:32:27.590 --> 00:32:31.230 align:middle line:90%
without victims in a sense.

00:32:31.230 --> 00:32:33.170 align:middle line:90%
So one of his--

00:32:33.170 --> 00:32:36.740 align:middle line:84%
anything that violates
a convention or a taboo

00:32:36.740 --> 00:32:39.770 align:middle line:90%
or generates discuss--

00:32:39.770 --> 00:32:42.920 align:middle line:84%
one of his favorite examples,
if you haven't read the book,

00:32:42.920 --> 00:32:49.670 align:middle line:84%
is about your favorite
dog runs out of the house

00:32:49.670 --> 00:32:53.500 align:middle line:90%
and is run over by a car.

00:32:53.500 --> 00:32:54.750 align:middle line:90%
The dog is dead.

00:32:54.750 --> 00:32:56.330 align:middle line:90%
It's your favorite dog.

00:32:56.330 --> 00:32:58.040 align:middle line:90%
So you are very sad.

00:32:58.040 --> 00:33:03.670 align:middle line:84%
You take the dog, you cook
the dog, and you eat your dog.

00:33:03.670 --> 00:33:08.650 align:middle line:84%
Now, you might say,
there is no harm.

00:33:08.650 --> 00:33:10.380 align:middle line:90%
The dog is dead.

00:33:10.380 --> 00:33:12.710 align:middle line:90%
Why don't you eat it?

00:33:12.710 --> 00:33:17.296 align:middle line:84%
Now, many people will view that
as being totally disgusting.

00:33:17.296 --> 00:33:19.914 align:middle line:90%


00:33:19.914 --> 00:33:21.850 align:middle line:90%
And that's the kind of thing.

00:33:21.850 --> 00:33:24.760 align:middle line:90%
And many things about--

00:33:24.760 --> 00:33:29.830 align:middle line:84%
the more serious examples are
things like duty, authority,

00:33:29.830 --> 00:33:31.960 align:middle line:84%
loyalty to the
in-group and so on,

00:33:31.960 --> 00:33:35.170 align:middle line:84%
which are deep values
for many people.

00:33:35.170 --> 00:33:38.950 align:middle line:84%
Now, an economist will not think
of those as being deep values.

00:33:38.950 --> 00:33:41.710 align:middle line:84%
They might think of that
as being an instrument.

00:33:41.710 --> 00:33:43.900 align:middle line:84%
So, sure, loyalty
may be important,

00:33:43.900 --> 00:33:46.330 align:middle line:84%
but that's an instrument
to sustain cooperation,

00:33:46.330 --> 00:33:48.280 align:middle line:90%
for example.

00:33:48.280 --> 00:33:51.340 align:middle line:84%
Same thing-- authority might be
an efficiency enhancing stuff,

00:33:51.340 --> 00:33:55.720 align:middle line:84%
but we would not value authority
per se as an economist.

00:33:55.720 --> 00:33:58.310 align:middle line:90%
But many people do.

00:33:58.310 --> 00:34:01.990 align:middle line:84%
And that's the kind
of thing that--

00:34:01.990 --> 00:34:05.050 align:middle line:84%
and that raises the more
general issue about,

00:34:05.050 --> 00:34:08.080 align:middle line:90%
what do we mean by harm?

00:34:08.080 --> 00:34:11.710 align:middle line:84%
So and that's something
which is very important now

00:34:11.710 --> 00:34:14.449 align:middle line:90%
in universities.

00:34:14.449 --> 00:34:19.679 align:middle line:84%
If I tell you something you
don't like, is that aggressive?

00:34:19.679 --> 00:34:24.318 align:middle line:90%


00:34:24.318 --> 00:34:29.429 align:middle line:84%
So of course, if it's an insult,
something racist or something

00:34:29.429 --> 00:34:30.780 align:middle line:90%
like that, of course it is.

00:34:30.780 --> 00:34:37.080 align:middle line:84%
But if it's an opinion,
which is not your opinion,

00:34:37.080 --> 00:34:38.670 align:middle line:90%
is that aggressive?

00:34:38.670 --> 00:34:46.230 align:middle line:84%
So that's really a big
thing and huge debate

00:34:46.230 --> 00:34:50.130 align:middle line:90%
now on exactly those things.

00:34:50.130 --> 00:34:51.900 align:middle line:90%
So let me--

00:34:51.900 --> 00:34:54.389 align:middle line:84%
I mean, we could talk
for hours about that.

00:34:54.389 --> 00:34:58.970 align:middle line:90%


00:34:58.970 --> 00:35:03.990 align:middle line:84%
Do you have any views on
that or any questions?

00:35:03.990 --> 00:35:08.358 align:middle line:90%


00:35:08.358 --> 00:35:09.900 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: How much
of morality do you

00:35:09.900 --> 00:35:13.080 align:middle line:90%
think is formed [INAUDIBLE].

00:35:13.080 --> 00:35:15.322 align:middle line:90%


00:35:15.322 --> 00:35:16.280 align:middle line:90%
JEAN TIROLE: I'm sorry.

00:35:16.280 --> 00:35:17.090 align:middle line:90%
How much?

00:35:17.090 --> 00:35:19.400 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: How much is
determined by society

00:35:19.400 --> 00:35:23.700 align:middle line:84%
and how much is by
some innate nature?

00:35:23.700 --> 00:35:26.120 align:middle line:84%
JEAN TIROLE: I guess
it's pretty unknown.

00:35:26.120 --> 00:35:28.650 align:middle line:90%


00:35:28.650 --> 00:35:30.840 align:middle line:84%
I mean, society plays
a big role, of course,

00:35:30.840 --> 00:35:32.140 align:middle line:90%
because of social norms.

00:35:32.140 --> 00:35:35.670 align:middle line:84%
And as we see from
some of these examples,

00:35:35.670 --> 00:35:38.280 align:middle line:84%
they fluctuate a lot
across time, for example,

00:35:38.280 --> 00:35:40.500 align:middle line:90%
or across culture.

00:35:40.500 --> 00:35:42.806 align:middle line:84%
So the social norms
are very important.

00:35:42.806 --> 00:35:45.470 align:middle line:90%


00:35:45.470 --> 00:35:50.730 align:middle line:90%
Now, it's hard to say.

00:35:50.730 --> 00:35:53.110 align:middle line:84%
Of course, we learn
that actually punching

00:35:53.110 --> 00:35:55.310 align:middle line:84%
the other child is not
a nice thing to do.

00:35:55.310 --> 00:35:58.878 align:middle line:90%


00:35:58.878 --> 00:36:04.340 align:middle line:84%
Now, is that innate
or is that society?

00:36:04.340 --> 00:36:09.200 align:middle line:84%
Well, you probably would have
learned it by yourself anyway,

00:36:09.200 --> 00:36:12.040 align:middle line:90%
but I don't know.

00:36:12.040 --> 00:36:15.499 align:middle line:90%


00:36:15.499 --> 00:36:19.500 align:middle line:84%
I mean, we still
are a social species

00:36:19.500 --> 00:36:23.220 align:middle line:84%
and a cooperative
species, by and large.

00:36:23.220 --> 00:36:28.300 align:middle line:84%
So how much do we learn
through epigenetics?

00:36:28.300 --> 00:36:29.050 align:middle line:90%
I don't know.

00:36:29.050 --> 00:36:31.980 align:middle line:90%
I mean, anyone knows here?

00:36:31.980 --> 00:36:32.480 align:middle line:90%
No.

00:36:32.480 --> 00:36:35.720 align:middle line:90%


00:36:35.720 --> 00:36:38.690 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So I guess
that kind of changes how

00:36:38.690 --> 00:36:42.424 align:middle line:90%
you think of the intervention.

00:36:42.424 --> 00:36:45.770 align:middle line:90%
Because if it's innate then--

00:36:45.770 --> 00:36:49.220 align:middle line:84%
JEAN TIROLE: Yeah, it becomes
more and more difficult.

00:36:49.220 --> 00:36:51.860 align:middle line:84%
But I would guess much
of it is not innate

00:36:51.860 --> 00:36:54.350 align:middle line:84%
because you see the
difference across culture

00:36:54.350 --> 00:36:58.520 align:middle line:84%
and across time,
that actually what

00:36:58.520 --> 00:37:02.220 align:middle line:84%
is being considered
moral in one society

00:37:02.220 --> 00:37:03.550 align:middle line:90%
may not be in another society.

00:37:03.550 --> 00:37:06.355 align:middle line:90%


00:37:06.355 --> 00:37:08.980 align:middle line:84%
And that's why I was
saying, do no harm.

00:37:08.980 --> 00:37:11.990 align:middle line:90%
I'm more kind of universal.

00:37:11.990 --> 00:37:17.340 align:middle line:84%
Everybody thinks that
committing a crime, for example,

00:37:17.340 --> 00:37:17.990 align:middle line:90%
is not moral.

00:37:17.990 --> 00:37:21.184 align:middle line:90%


00:37:21.184 --> 00:37:24.610 align:middle line:90%
I mean, even pollution.

00:37:24.610 --> 00:37:27.670 align:middle line:84%
Nobody will say it's
great to pollute.

00:37:27.670 --> 00:37:30.190 align:middle line:84%
Even those who don't
care, they will not

00:37:30.190 --> 00:37:34.060 align:middle line:84%
claim the moral high ground and
say it's wonderful to pollute.

00:37:34.060 --> 00:37:34.880 align:middle line:90%
No, no, no.

00:37:34.880 --> 00:37:37.600 align:middle line:90%


00:37:37.600 --> 00:37:41.045 align:middle line:84%
Those are relatively
consensual issues in a sense.

00:37:41.045 --> 00:37:42.910 align:middle line:84%
We may disagree
about the importance,

00:37:42.910 --> 00:37:45.510 align:middle line:90%
but the sign is clear.

00:37:45.510 --> 00:37:51.280 align:middle line:90%


00:37:51.280 --> 00:37:51.780 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:37:51.780 --> 00:37:54.980 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So going back to
the point on competition.

00:37:54.980 --> 00:37:57.680 align:middle line:84%
So is there a sense in which
having more competition

00:37:57.680 --> 00:38:01.500 align:middle line:84%
can actually be a bad
thing if the result is--

00:38:01.500 --> 00:38:04.340 align:middle line:84%
JEAN TIROLE: That's what
we're going to study now.

00:38:04.340 --> 00:38:08.570 align:middle line:84%
Because that's the claim, is
that competition basically takes

00:38:08.570 --> 00:38:11.030 align:middle line:90%
the morality out of people.

00:38:11.030 --> 00:38:13.190 align:middle line:84%
So the managers or
the shareholders,

00:38:13.190 --> 00:38:16.507 align:middle line:84%
they will behave better if they
were in a non-compete world.

00:38:16.507 --> 00:38:18.590 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: But that doesn't
include the possibility

00:38:18.590 --> 00:38:21.200 align:middle line:84%
that, if there was no
competition, that firms

00:38:21.200 --> 00:38:25.082 align:middle line:84%
would still do immoral things?
it's just to purely seek profit.

00:38:25.082 --> 00:38:26.790 align:middle line:84%
JEAN TIROLE: That
would be a possibility.

00:38:26.790 --> 00:38:29.210 align:middle line:84%
So we are going to
look at an environment

00:38:29.210 --> 00:38:33.710 align:middle line:84%
where people have
moral preferences.

00:38:33.710 --> 00:38:36.290 align:middle line:84%
So they care about their
money, but they also

00:38:36.290 --> 00:38:38.300 align:middle line:90%
care about other things.

00:38:38.300 --> 00:38:43.580 align:middle line:84%
Which, I think we all are
like that to some extent,

00:38:43.580 --> 00:38:45.150 align:middle line:90%
to different extents, of course.

00:38:45.150 --> 00:38:48.080 align:middle line:84%
But we care about the
money, but we also

00:38:48.080 --> 00:38:49.970 align:middle line:84%
care about doing
the right thing,

00:38:49.970 --> 00:38:52.045 align:middle line:84%
with different
intensities of course.

00:38:52.045 --> 00:38:55.936 align:middle line:90%


00:38:55.936 --> 00:39:01.420 align:middle line:84%
So what we are going to see is
that the nature of competition

00:39:01.420 --> 00:39:03.410 align:middle line:84%
affects market
morality in many ways.

00:39:03.410 --> 00:39:06.950 align:middle line:84%
So it's going to depend on
the intensity of competition,

00:39:06.950 --> 00:39:10.690 align:middle line:84%
so market structure, the number
of firms and substitutability,

00:39:10.690 --> 00:39:13.030 align:middle line:90%
which are standard IO things.

00:39:13.030 --> 00:39:18.030 align:middle line:84%
But it's going to depend
also on whether prices

00:39:18.030 --> 00:39:20.140 align:middle line:90%
are flexible or fixed.

00:39:20.140 --> 00:39:23.500 align:middle line:84%
You are going to tell me,
prices are always flexible.

00:39:23.500 --> 00:39:27.170 align:middle line:84%
Well, not quite, because
sometimes your price is fixed.

00:39:27.170 --> 00:39:32.200 align:middle line:84%
So if you are a doctor in
many countries or a notary

00:39:32.200 --> 00:39:36.250 align:middle line:84%
or whatever, you get some
fixed amount of money--

00:39:36.250 --> 00:39:37.390 align:middle line:90%
or a taxi or--

00:39:37.390 --> 00:39:40.000 align:middle line:90%


00:39:40.000 --> 00:39:42.970 align:middle line:84%
you don't have
price flexibility.

00:39:42.970 --> 00:39:46.750 align:middle line:84%
In franchising
environment, your price

00:39:46.750 --> 00:39:49.520 align:middle line:84%
may be determined by
the franchiser anyway.

00:39:49.520 --> 00:39:53.020 align:middle line:84%
So sometimes the
prices are fixed,

00:39:53.020 --> 00:39:56.540 align:middle line:84%
but the normal thing
is flexible prices.

00:39:56.540 --> 00:39:58.400 align:middle line:84%
Then we'll see it's
important to know

00:39:58.400 --> 00:40:03.470 align:middle line:84%
whether cutting ethical corners
lowers cost or boosts demand.

00:40:03.470 --> 00:40:06.860 align:middle line:84%
By the way, we are going
to look at a situation

00:40:06.860 --> 00:40:09.980 align:middle line:84%
where all stakeholders can
have moral preferences.

00:40:09.980 --> 00:40:12.960 align:middle line:84%
There can be zero, but
there can be also positive.

00:40:12.960 --> 00:40:17.330 align:middle line:84%
So the stakeholders are, of
course, you have the managers,

00:40:17.330 --> 00:40:22.590 align:middle line:84%
but you also have the consumers,
the investors, the workers.

00:40:22.590 --> 00:40:24.750 align:middle line:90%
So here is a picture.

00:40:24.750 --> 00:40:29.240 align:middle line:84%
You will have an
oligopoly with n firms.

00:40:29.240 --> 00:40:33.830 align:middle line:84%
Those firms will set a
price, except if the prices

00:40:33.830 --> 00:40:35.480 align:middle line:90%
are regulated.

00:40:35.480 --> 00:40:38.645 align:middle line:84%
And they will choose
a morality of their--

00:40:38.645 --> 00:40:41.030 align:middle line:84%
a morality technology,
if you want.

00:40:41.030 --> 00:40:44.510 align:middle line:84%
So they can choose
something that pollutes

00:40:44.510 --> 00:40:46.850 align:middle line:90%
a lot or pollutes little.

00:40:46.850 --> 00:40:49.850 align:middle line:84%
They may be nice
with their workers.

00:40:49.850 --> 00:40:53.270 align:middle line:84%
They may misrepresent
their product.

00:40:53.270 --> 00:40:59.030 align:middle line:84%
They may-- all kinds of issues
which creates anxiety on people

00:40:59.030 --> 00:41:00.700 align:middle line:90%
and raises a moral issue.

00:41:00.700 --> 00:41:03.560 align:middle line:90%


00:41:03.560 --> 00:41:07.380 align:middle line:90%
They will sell to the consumers.

00:41:07.380 --> 00:41:12.120 align:middle line:84%
And there are input suppliers,
like workers and investors,

00:41:12.120 --> 00:41:15.245 align:middle line:84%
that bring labor and
capital to the firm.

00:41:15.245 --> 00:41:19.010 align:middle line:90%


00:41:19.010 --> 00:41:21.650 align:middle line:90%
Now, those are stakeholders.

00:41:21.650 --> 00:41:26.270 align:middle line:84%
So they may care, actually,
about the moral action

00:41:26.270 --> 00:41:29.510 align:middle line:84%
or the ethical actions which
are chosen by the firms.

00:41:29.510 --> 00:41:31.430 align:middle line:84%
So a consumer,
for example, might

00:41:31.430 --> 00:41:35.510 align:middle line:84%
want to buy fair trade coffee
or whatever, might actually

00:41:35.510 --> 00:41:40.790 align:middle line:84%
want to have more production
for their consumptions.

00:41:40.790 --> 00:41:43.350 align:middle line:84%
Same thing-- the
workers may care.

00:41:43.350 --> 00:41:47.210 align:middle line:84%
They may want to work
for an ethical firm.

00:41:47.210 --> 00:41:50.030 align:middle line:90%
By the way, if you look at--

00:41:50.030 --> 00:41:52.250 align:middle line:84%
there is a premium,
actually, which

00:41:52.250 --> 00:41:56.460 align:middle line:84%
is paid to workers working
in, say, the tobacco industry.

00:41:56.460 --> 00:42:00.780 align:middle line:84%
So if you work in an industry
which is considered as immoral,

00:42:00.780 --> 00:42:02.400 align:middle line:90%
you have a higher wage--

00:42:02.400 --> 00:42:05.490 align:middle line:90%
or casino or whatever.

00:42:05.490 --> 00:42:07.123 align:middle line:90%
Same thing for investors.

00:42:07.123 --> 00:42:08.790 align:middle line:84%
The investors, they
might want to engage

00:42:08.790 --> 00:42:12.800 align:middle line:84%
in socially-responsible
investment.

00:42:12.800 --> 00:42:18.790 align:middle line:84%
Now, the key question,
as we are going to see,

00:42:18.790 --> 00:42:23.240 align:middle line:84%
is whether unethical behavior
raises or lowers demand.

00:42:23.240 --> 00:42:25.880 align:middle line:84%
Because if social
responsibility,

00:42:25.880 --> 00:42:31.230 align:middle line:84%
as we're going to see, goes
through the cost structure,

00:42:31.230 --> 00:42:34.500 align:middle line:84%
then it's not going to
have any competition.

00:42:34.500 --> 00:42:37.470 align:middle line:84%
It's not going to have an
impact because competition

00:42:37.470 --> 00:42:40.770 align:middle line:84%
is going to operate
through the demand curve,

00:42:40.770 --> 00:42:42.095 align:middle line:90%
not on the cost side.

00:42:42.095 --> 00:42:47.235 align:middle line:90%


00:42:47.235 --> 00:42:49.400 align:middle line:84%
So we are going to see
that's going to be.

00:42:49.400 --> 00:42:51.290 align:middle line:84%
This is pretty
unclear at this stage.

00:42:51.290 --> 00:42:54.560 align:middle line:90%
So the model is the following.

00:42:54.560 --> 00:43:00.410 align:middle line:84%
Oligopoly of n firms serving
unit-demand consumers, say.

00:43:00.410 --> 00:43:02.030 align:middle line:90%
The firm chooses a price.

00:43:02.030 --> 00:43:08.150 align:middle line:84%
Firm i chooses a price, p i,
and an ethical action, ai.

00:43:08.150 --> 00:43:13.150 align:middle line:84%
So think of ai equals 0 as the
most immoral action, so you

00:43:13.150 --> 00:43:16.133 align:middle line:90%
pollute maximally, for example.

00:43:16.133 --> 00:43:21.790 align:middle line:90%


00:43:21.790 --> 00:43:25.090 align:middle line:90%
You sell to any dictator.

00:43:25.090 --> 00:43:27.070 align:middle line:84%
You sell weapons
to any dictator.

00:43:27.070 --> 00:43:30.010 align:middle line:90%
And that creates some--

00:43:30.010 --> 00:43:31.820 align:middle line:90%
that has a welfare impact--

00:43:31.820 --> 00:43:34.960 align:middle line:84%
think of that as
being an externality--

00:43:34.960 --> 00:43:39.790 align:middle line:84%
a welfare impact, wi
of ai per unit of--

00:43:39.790 --> 00:43:43.030 align:middle line:84%
So you might think, for example,
that might be the pollution

00:43:43.030 --> 00:43:47.410 align:middle line:90%
damage from emitting carbon.

00:43:47.410 --> 00:43:52.060 align:middle line:84%
It might be the damage done
by the dictator with a weapon.

00:43:52.060 --> 00:43:54.210 align:middle line:90%
So that kind of thing.

00:43:54.210 --> 00:43:55.860 align:middle line:90%
And it's increasing ai.

00:43:55.860 --> 00:44:02.310 align:middle line:84%
So [? i or ?] ai would be a more
moral action just by-- it's just

00:44:02.310 --> 00:44:03.600 align:middle line:90%
a normalization.

00:44:03.600 --> 00:44:10.880 align:middle line:84%
ai will belong to some interval,
with 0 being the lower bound.

00:44:10.880 --> 00:44:15.560 align:middle line:84%
And the welfare impact will
be-- so it will be increasing,

00:44:15.560 --> 00:44:16.873 align:middle line:90%
and it will be concave.

00:44:16.873 --> 00:44:20.790 align:middle line:90%


00:44:20.790 --> 00:44:24.580 align:middle line:84%
The moral action may actually
also affect the unit cost.

00:44:24.580 --> 00:44:28.260 align:middle line:84%
So the unit cost of production,
which will be a constant,

00:44:28.260 --> 00:44:31.230 align:middle line:90%
will be ci of ai.

00:44:31.230 --> 00:44:35.990 align:middle line:84%
Now, you can consider
two cases, one in which

00:44:35.990 --> 00:44:40.800 align:middle line:84%
this unit cost is increasing or
decreasing with immoral action.

00:44:40.800 --> 00:44:44.950 align:middle line:84%
So if you think about
the moral action is not

00:44:44.950 --> 00:44:50.000 align:middle line:84%
using child labor,
not using fossil fuel,

00:44:50.000 --> 00:44:53.020 align:middle line:90%
then it's expensive.

00:44:53.020 --> 00:44:56.770 align:middle line:84%
I mean, people use
children, they use carbon,

00:44:56.770 --> 00:44:59.930 align:middle line:84%
they use coal
because it's cheap.

00:44:59.930 --> 00:45:02.720 align:middle line:84%
Otherwise, they will
not be doing it.

00:45:02.720 --> 00:45:04.720 align:middle line:90%
They use it because it's cheap.

00:45:04.720 --> 00:45:06.940 align:middle line:84%
So in that case, if you
behave more morally,

00:45:06.940 --> 00:45:09.590 align:middle line:90%
your unit cost goes up.

00:45:09.590 --> 00:45:12.530 align:middle line:84%
But there are also cases in
which, when you behave morally,

00:45:12.530 --> 00:45:15.710 align:middle line:90%
your unit cost goes down.

00:45:15.710 --> 00:45:17.810 align:middle line:90%
So that's a case--

00:45:17.810 --> 00:45:20.540 align:middle line:84%
coming back to the
previous slide--

00:45:20.540 --> 00:45:25.760 align:middle line:84%
if you have responsible input
suppliers-- so basically,

00:45:25.760 --> 00:45:29.330 align:middle line:84%
workers or investors
with social preferences--

00:45:29.330 --> 00:45:32.330 align:middle line:84%
then they are
willing to actually

00:45:32.330 --> 00:45:36.230 align:middle line:84%
have a lower wage or a lower
return on their capital

00:45:36.230 --> 00:45:41.450 align:middle line:84%
in order to be associated with
a firm which behaves morally.

00:45:41.450 --> 00:45:46.850 align:middle line:84%
So in that case, if you
behave more morally,

00:45:46.850 --> 00:45:51.830 align:middle line:84%
your input supplier actually
offers you a discount

00:45:51.830 --> 00:45:53.640 align:middle line:84%
because they have
social preferences.

00:45:53.640 --> 00:45:57.140 align:middle line:84%
So in practice, you
really have both.

00:45:57.140 --> 00:45:59.690 align:middle line:84%
So we are going to be
agnostic at this stage

00:45:59.690 --> 00:46:03.140 align:middle line:84%
about whether the moral action
actually increases or decreases

00:46:03.140 --> 00:46:03.660 align:middle line:90%
the cost.

00:46:03.660 --> 00:46:07.350 align:middle line:90%
That depends on the situation.

00:46:07.350 --> 00:46:07.945 align:middle line:90%
Is that clear?

00:46:07.945 --> 00:46:11.820 align:middle line:90%


00:46:11.820 --> 00:46:14.850 align:middle line:84%
Now, a key concept for
what's going to follow

00:46:14.850 --> 00:46:16.410 align:middle line:90%
is the net price.

00:46:16.410 --> 00:46:19.200 align:middle line:84%
Consumers care about the
price, and they consider

00:46:19.200 --> 00:46:22.000 align:middle line:90%
about the ethical behavior.

00:46:22.000 --> 00:46:26.230 align:middle line:84%
So they also will have
social preferences.

00:46:26.230 --> 00:46:28.370 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to come
to that in a minute.

00:46:28.370 --> 00:46:31.580 align:middle line:84%
So everything for
them will be as

00:46:31.580 --> 00:46:39.390 align:middle line:84%
if they care about the real
price p i, the nominal price.

00:46:39.390 --> 00:46:44.760 align:middle line:84%
And then they will have some
preferences over the action--

00:46:44.760 --> 00:46:46.490 align:middle line:90%
the morality of the action.

00:46:46.490 --> 00:46:49.607 align:middle line:84%
Now, this phi i can be also
increasing or decreasing

00:46:49.607 --> 00:46:50.940 align:middle line:90%
with the morality of the action.

00:46:50.940 --> 00:46:58.260 align:middle line:84%
So usually consumers
prefer a more moral action.

00:46:58.260 --> 00:47:00.850 align:middle line:84%
They are socially
responsible consumers.

00:47:00.850 --> 00:47:02.790 align:middle line:90%
They want fair trade coffee.

00:47:02.790 --> 00:47:09.120 align:middle line:84%
They want workers' rights
to be respected and so on.

00:47:09.120 --> 00:47:11.360 align:middle line:84%
But they're also--
so in that case,

00:47:11.360 --> 00:47:17.000 align:middle line:84%
phi i is decreasing in
ai because that's as

00:47:17.000 --> 00:47:19.040 align:middle line:90%
if the price were going down.

00:47:19.040 --> 00:47:22.610 align:middle line:84%
But in the other
example I gave you

00:47:22.610 --> 00:47:27.470 align:middle line:84%
of bribing a foreign official,
selling weapons to a dictator,

00:47:27.470 --> 00:47:34.940 align:middle line:84%
giving more opioids, and
so on, there, basically,

00:47:34.940 --> 00:47:36.920 align:middle line:90%
phi i is increasing in ai.

00:47:36.920 --> 00:47:39.410 align:middle line:84%
Because if you choose
more moral actions,

00:47:39.410 --> 00:47:44.090 align:middle line:84%
that means you are reluctant to
sell weapons to the dictator,

00:47:44.090 --> 00:47:47.790 align:middle line:84%
you are more reluctant
to give opioids,

00:47:47.790 --> 00:47:51.560 align:middle line:84%
and that's going
to reduce demand.

00:47:51.560 --> 00:47:54.400 align:middle line:90%
So the same thing.

00:47:54.400 --> 00:47:56.680 align:middle line:84%
The moral action can
actually increase demand

00:47:56.680 --> 00:48:00.060 align:middle line:84%
or reduce demand, but not
in the same circumstances.

00:48:00.060 --> 00:48:03.250 align:middle line:84%
So for fair trade
coffee, more action

00:48:03.250 --> 00:48:04.555 align:middle line:90%
is going to increase demand.

00:48:04.555 --> 00:48:08.638 align:middle line:90%


00:48:08.638 --> 00:48:13.570 align:middle line:84%
For the example I gave you with
replacement logic, actually,

00:48:13.570 --> 00:48:16.990 align:middle line:84%
immoral action is
going to reduce demand.

00:48:16.990 --> 00:48:20.020 align:middle line:84%
And that's going to be
completely crucial for what's

00:48:20.020 --> 00:48:22.440 align:middle line:90%
going to happen.

00:48:22.440 --> 00:48:24.220 align:middle line:90%
There will be an outside option.

00:48:24.220 --> 00:48:26.160 align:middle line:84%
So the consumer
chooses between one

00:48:26.160 --> 00:48:28.810 align:middle line:84%
of the firms and
the outside option.

00:48:28.810 --> 00:48:34.410 align:middle line:84%
The outside option will
have an a0 and a p0.

00:48:34.410 --> 00:48:36.040 align:middle line:90%
And p0 is often 0.

00:48:36.040 --> 00:48:39.030 align:middle line:84%
So if you don't consume
it, it might be 0.

00:48:39.030 --> 00:48:42.630 align:middle line:84%
But still, if your
option is actually

00:48:42.630 --> 00:48:47.040 align:middle line:84%
to reopen the coal
mines, then p0 is small,

00:48:47.040 --> 00:48:48.950 align:middle line:90%
but a0 is also very small.

00:48:48.950 --> 00:48:53.750 align:middle line:90%


00:48:53.750 --> 00:48:58.410 align:middle line:90%
You have a vector of net prices.

00:48:58.410 --> 00:49:01.830 align:middle line:84%
And those net prices are
going to determine the demand.

00:49:01.830 --> 00:49:06.660 align:middle line:84%
So the demand for firm i will
depend on that price of firm i

00:49:06.660 --> 00:49:10.530 align:middle line:84%
and the net prices
of the rivals.

00:49:10.530 --> 00:49:12.215 align:middle line:90%
There are substitute goods.

00:49:12.215 --> 00:49:15.018 align:middle line:90%


00:49:15.018 --> 00:49:22.310 align:middle line:84%
So basically, my demand
increases with your price

00:49:22.310 --> 00:49:27.080 align:middle line:84%
and decreases with my price,
the standard assumption.

00:49:27.080 --> 00:49:30.080 align:middle line:84%
So you can think about
the discrete choice

00:49:30.080 --> 00:49:36.200 align:middle line:84%
model, in which,
basically, there are

00:49:36.200 --> 00:49:38.060 align:middle line:90%
valuations which are drawn--

00:49:38.060 --> 00:49:41.420 align:middle line:84%
including for the outside
option, by the way--

00:49:41.420 --> 00:49:43.860 align:middle line:84%
and you choose which
option you prefer.

00:49:43.860 --> 00:49:48.055 align:middle line:84%
And that's one way of
generating such a demand.

00:49:48.055 --> 00:49:52.196 align:middle line:90%


00:49:52.196 --> 00:49:57.150 align:middle line:84%
So we are going to call eta
i the elasticity of demand

00:49:57.150 --> 00:49:58.360 align:middle line:90%
for firm i.

00:49:58.360 --> 00:50:04.230 align:middle line:84%
So it's dDi over dpi, or
dpi hat-- it's the same

00:50:04.230 --> 00:50:08.940 align:middle line:84%
because p i hat is
equal to p i plus phi i.

00:50:08.940 --> 00:50:11.320 align:middle line:84%
So that's the standard
density of demand.

00:50:11.320 --> 00:50:17.770 align:middle line:84%
We make the standard assumption
that when prices go up,

00:50:17.770 --> 00:50:21.290 align:middle line:84%
the elasticity of
demand decreases.

00:50:21.290 --> 00:50:28.800 align:middle line:84%
It's almost the same assumption
as saying the goods are

00:50:28.800 --> 00:50:31.410 align:middle line:90%
strategic complements.

00:50:31.410 --> 00:50:34.140 align:middle line:84%
So it's strategic
complementarity assumption,

00:50:34.140 --> 00:50:35.710 align:middle line:90%
basically.

00:50:35.710 --> 00:50:38.260 align:middle line:84%
So you assume that
for higher prices,

00:50:38.260 --> 00:50:41.740 align:middle line:84%
the elasticities are
lower, which is standard.

00:50:41.740 --> 00:50:43.960 align:middle line:84%
But it's also standard
just to assume

00:50:43.960 --> 00:50:48.590 align:middle line:84%
that, basically, the goods
are strategic complements.

00:50:48.590 --> 00:50:54.000 align:middle line:90%


00:50:54.000 --> 00:50:56.710 align:middle line:90%
So the assumptions I make are--

00:50:56.710 --> 00:51:00.060 align:middle line:84%
So what's going to play
a big role is this.

00:51:00.060 --> 00:51:02.990 align:middle line:90%


00:51:02.990 --> 00:51:08.740 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to say that
when demand increases

00:51:08.740 --> 00:51:12.500 align:middle line:84%
with the morality of the action,
that means the consumers are

00:51:12.500 --> 00:51:14.950 align:middle line:90%
socially responsible.

00:51:14.950 --> 00:51:21.780 align:middle line:84%
When demand decreases
with the moral action,

00:51:21.780 --> 00:51:24.670 align:middle line:84%
then the consumers are
socially irresponsible.

00:51:24.670 --> 00:51:28.230 align:middle line:84%
So all of the examples I gave
you on the first slide were

00:51:28.230 --> 00:51:31.000 align:middle line:84%
examples with socially
irresponsible consumers--

00:51:31.000 --> 00:51:36.090 align:middle line:84%
the dictator, the
consumer of the opioids,

00:51:36.090 --> 00:51:40.500 align:middle line:84%
the official who gets bribed,
the athlete who actually wants

00:51:40.500 --> 00:51:41.535 align:middle line:90%
a drug--

00:51:41.535 --> 00:51:46.410 align:middle line:84%
a performance-enhancing
drug, and so on.

00:51:46.410 --> 00:51:49.620 align:middle line:84%
So socially irresponsible
consumer, phi i prime

00:51:49.620 --> 00:51:50.740 align:middle line:90%
is negative.

00:51:50.740 --> 00:51:53.790 align:middle line:84%
Socially responsible
consumer means

00:51:53.790 --> 00:51:55.710 align:middle line:90%
that phi i prime is positive.

00:51:55.710 --> 00:51:58.530 align:middle line:84%
So, for example, one of the
things that might happen

00:51:58.530 --> 00:52:00.240 align:middle line:90%
is that you internalize--

00:52:00.240 --> 00:52:02.280 align:middle line:84%
and this is going to
be our first example

00:52:02.280 --> 00:52:08.580 align:middle line:84%
of social preferences-- you
internalize the welfare impact

00:52:08.580 --> 00:52:09.690 align:middle line:90%
of your action.

00:52:09.690 --> 00:52:14.080 align:middle line:84%
So think about that as being the
cause of pollution, for example.

00:52:14.080 --> 00:52:19.695 align:middle line:84%
Then, you put a weight, alpha c,
on the cost of your pollution,

00:52:19.695 --> 00:52:21.320 align:middle line:84%
which, in general,
would be less than 1

00:52:21.320 --> 00:52:23.510 align:middle line:84%
because you never
fully internalize

00:52:23.510 --> 00:52:24.640 align:middle line:90%
the impact on others.

00:52:24.640 --> 00:52:27.200 align:middle line:90%


00:52:27.200 --> 00:52:29.900 align:middle line:84%
Now, you have this
strange case in which--

00:52:29.900 --> 00:52:33.080 align:middle line:84%
I mean, they look like Homo
economicus, of course--

00:52:33.080 --> 00:52:37.250 align:middle line:84%
in which the consumers don't
care about the moral action

00:52:37.250 --> 00:52:39.980 align:middle line:84%
or, I should say,
rather they may not

00:52:39.980 --> 00:52:41.060 align:middle line:90%
observe the moral action.

00:52:41.060 --> 00:52:43.460 align:middle line:84%
Because there are
also cases in which

00:52:43.460 --> 00:52:46.740 align:middle line:84%
you don't observe the
moral action of the firm.

00:52:46.740 --> 00:52:50.150 align:middle line:84%
In that case, their demand
doesn't react to immoral action

00:52:50.150 --> 00:52:51.780 align:middle line:90%
because they don't observe it.

00:52:51.780 --> 00:52:54.310 align:middle line:84%
And they are kind
of socially neutral.

00:52:54.310 --> 00:52:58.510 align:middle line:90%


00:52:58.510 --> 00:53:04.030 align:middle line:84%
Do you have questions
about the framework?

00:53:04.030 --> 00:53:04.660 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:53:04.660 --> 00:53:05.680 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Sure.

00:53:05.680 --> 00:53:09.130 align:middle line:84%
How restricted, if
at all, is it to have

00:53:09.130 --> 00:53:12.320 align:middle line:84%
the morality term entering
through the price like that?

00:53:12.320 --> 00:53:18.250 align:middle line:84%
JEAN TIROLE: Yeah, so
the additivity property.

00:53:18.250 --> 00:53:21.530 align:middle line:84%
So the idea is that, in a sense,
there is no income effect.

00:53:21.530 --> 00:53:31.770 align:middle line:84%
So you pay a price, and then you
get some benefit or some cost

00:53:31.770 --> 00:53:35.238 align:middle line:90%
of the immoral action.

00:53:35.238 --> 00:53:42.960 align:middle line:84%
So it's true if this particular
purchase were really big

00:53:42.960 --> 00:53:46.680 align:middle line:84%
compared to the income of the
person, that might not hold,

00:53:46.680 --> 00:53:48.690 align:middle line:90%
for example.

00:53:48.690 --> 00:53:53.610 align:middle line:84%
So think about a bribe, for
example, for an official.

00:53:53.610 --> 00:53:56.590 align:middle line:84%
So if the official gets
lots of small bribes,

00:53:56.590 --> 00:53:58.770 align:middle line:90%
that's not an issue.

00:53:58.770 --> 00:54:04.590 align:middle line:84%
But if that was a huge
bribe, which then there

00:54:04.590 --> 00:54:08.990 align:middle line:84%
might be some income
effect, which--

00:54:08.990 --> 00:54:11.840 align:middle line:84%
because then, that means
the marginal utility

00:54:11.840 --> 00:54:15.620 align:middle line:84%
of income, and therefore
the price, in a sense--

00:54:15.620 --> 00:54:19.910 align:middle line:84%
the impact of the price
will not be the same.

00:54:19.910 --> 00:54:23.290 align:middle line:84%
But it's pretty reasonable,
I would say, in general.

00:54:23.290 --> 00:54:30.822 align:middle line:90%


00:54:30.822 --> 00:54:31.780 align:middle line:90%
That's a good question.

00:54:31.780 --> 00:54:35.210 align:middle line:90%


00:54:35.210 --> 00:54:39.230 align:middle line:84%
OK, the cost side impact
of ethical behavior.

00:54:39.230 --> 00:54:44.420 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to assume--
it's more general than that,

00:54:44.420 --> 00:54:47.120 align:middle line:84%
actually, but we're just
going to assume that people

00:54:47.120 --> 00:54:48.140 align:middle line:90%
have social preferences.

00:54:48.140 --> 00:54:52.800 align:middle line:84%
So they internalize
Wi to some extent.

00:54:52.800 --> 00:54:58.520 align:middle line:84%
So just to normalize,
imagine that you

00:54:58.520 --> 00:55:00.830 align:middle line:84%
need one unit of capital
and one unit of labor

00:55:00.830 --> 00:55:04.100 align:middle line:90%
to produce one unit of good.

00:55:04.100 --> 00:55:08.585 align:middle line:84%
And then imagine that investors
are willing to forego alpha i--

00:55:08.585 --> 00:55:10.140 align:middle line:90%
i for investor--

00:55:10.140 --> 00:55:15.390 align:middle line:90%
Wi of ai in their return.

00:55:15.390 --> 00:55:20.290 align:middle line:84%
And same thing for the workers,
with a different weight.

00:55:20.290 --> 00:55:23.280 align:middle line:84%
So they are willing
to work for lower wage

00:55:23.280 --> 00:55:25.150 align:middle line:90%
if you are more moral.

00:55:25.150 --> 00:55:27.810 align:middle line:90%


00:55:27.810 --> 00:55:32.280 align:middle line:84%
Then, basically,
your marginal costs,

00:55:32.280 --> 00:55:34.200 align:middle line:84%
the way it changes
with the action

00:55:34.200 --> 00:55:39.080 align:middle line:84%
depends on the cost
of doing business.

00:55:39.080 --> 00:55:40.670 align:middle line:84%
So here, you might
have the impact

00:55:40.670 --> 00:55:44.240 align:middle line:84%
of child labor or pollution,
if you use a polluting--

00:55:44.240 --> 00:55:47.030 align:middle line:90%
a cheap but polluting input.

00:55:47.030 --> 00:55:50.810 align:middle line:84%
But then on the other side,
you might also have the fact

00:55:50.810 --> 00:55:58.850 align:middle line:84%
that workers and investors
are willing to actually get

00:55:58.850 --> 00:56:01.300 align:middle line:84%
a lower rate and a lower
return on their capital.

00:56:01.300 --> 00:56:06.640 align:middle line:90%


00:56:06.640 --> 00:56:10.320 align:middle line:84%
So I already said
that in a sense.

00:56:10.320 --> 00:56:12.970 align:middle line:84%
Demand impact of
supplier's moral choice.

00:56:12.970 --> 00:56:16.450 align:middle line:84%
You have irresponsible
consumers.

00:56:16.450 --> 00:56:18.300 align:middle line:84%
Now, in the paper,
or in the appendix,

00:56:18.300 --> 00:56:20.670 align:middle line:84%
we have some foundations
for the demand-side benefits

00:56:20.670 --> 00:56:22.040 align:middle line:90%
of cutting ethical corners.

00:56:22.040 --> 00:56:25.380 align:middle line:90%
It could be an externality.

00:56:25.380 --> 00:56:27.570 align:middle line:84%
That's the case
of those examples.

00:56:27.570 --> 00:56:30.100 align:middle line:84%
It could be an internality,
like an opioid.

00:56:30.100 --> 00:56:33.960 align:middle line:84%
It could also be all kinds
of shrouded attributes also.

00:56:33.960 --> 00:56:37.410 align:middle line:90%
That could also be the case.

00:56:37.410 --> 00:56:41.880 align:middle line:90%
And then responsible consumers.

00:56:41.880 --> 00:56:44.640 align:middle line:84%
I'm coming to the firm's
objective function

00:56:44.640 --> 00:56:48.240 align:middle line:84%
because that's-- so they
too have social preferences.

00:56:48.240 --> 00:56:50.110 align:middle line:90%
So they care about the profit.

00:56:50.110 --> 00:56:56.630 align:middle line:84%
The profit is price minus
unit cost times demand.

00:56:56.630 --> 00:56:58.340 align:middle line:90%
That's nothing new here.

00:56:58.340 --> 00:57:00.560 align:middle line:84%
But the difference
with a standard firm

00:57:00.560 --> 00:57:03.950 align:middle line:84%
is that they will have
social preferences,

00:57:03.950 --> 00:57:06.590 align:middle line:90%
so they care about the welfare.

00:57:06.590 --> 00:57:08.970 align:middle line:90%
Now, we can discuss that.

00:57:08.970 --> 00:57:10.790 align:middle line:90%
What is welfare?

00:57:10.790 --> 00:57:16.960 align:middle line:84%
Now, in the paper, we have
general consequentialist

00:57:16.960 --> 00:57:18.850 align:middle line:90%
preferences.

00:57:18.850 --> 00:57:23.950 align:middle line:84%
And I'm just going to give
you one example of that,

00:57:23.950 --> 00:57:29.114 align:middle line:84%
which can be criticized, but
it's much broader than that.

00:57:29.114 --> 00:57:35.320 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to assume, for
the sake of the argument,

00:57:35.320 --> 00:57:42.350 align:middle line:84%
that you internalize
basically the industry

00:57:42.350 --> 00:57:45.670 align:middle line:84%
pollution or the industry's
amount of opioids or something

00:57:45.670 --> 00:57:46.170 align:middle line:90%
like that.

00:57:46.170 --> 00:57:51.720 align:middle line:84%
So you take into
account the sum--

00:57:51.720 --> 00:57:54.690 align:middle line:84%
so basically, firm j
will get demand Dj.

00:57:54.690 --> 00:57:58.710 align:middle line:84%
A fraction of consumer
Dj will go to firm j.

00:57:58.710 --> 00:58:05.270 align:middle line:84%
The welfare consequence
of firm j is Wj of aj.

00:58:05.270 --> 00:58:08.060 align:middle line:84%
And you sum over
all firms and also

00:58:08.060 --> 00:58:12.630 align:middle line:84%
the outside option, possibly,
of those welfare consequences.

00:58:12.630 --> 00:58:16.160 align:middle line:84%
So you care, say, about the
total pollution in the industry

00:58:16.160 --> 00:58:18.980 align:middle line:84%
or the total amount
of the opioids

00:58:18.980 --> 00:58:20.500 align:middle line:90%
that are sold in the industry.

00:58:20.500 --> 00:58:24.995 align:middle line:90%


00:58:24.995 --> 00:58:29.750 align:middle line:84%
The class of consequentialists'
preferences is much broader.

00:58:29.750 --> 00:58:35.630 align:middle line:84%
So you might internalize, for
example, just your own Wi Di

00:58:35.630 --> 00:58:40.480 align:middle line:84%
so your own pollution,
in a sense, which

00:58:40.480 --> 00:58:42.860 align:middle line:90%
is harder to defend morally.

00:58:42.860 --> 00:58:47.230 align:middle line:84%
Because you have to take into
account also that if you sell

00:58:47.230 --> 00:58:49.510 align:middle line:84%
one more-- if you get
one more consumer,

00:58:49.510 --> 00:58:52.390 align:middle line:84%
that consumer will have
purchased elsewhere.

00:58:52.390 --> 00:58:55.210 align:middle line:84%
So morally, you
will say, no, no,

00:58:55.210 --> 00:58:57.160 align:middle line:84%
just looking at
your own pollution

00:58:57.160 --> 00:58:59.470 align:middle line:90%
makes no sense morally.

00:58:59.470 --> 00:59:00.490 align:middle line:90%
But that's OK.

00:59:00.490 --> 00:59:04.420 align:middle line:84%
For the theory,
you can allow it.

00:59:04.420 --> 00:59:07.210 align:middle line:84%
And there are other
social consequences

00:59:07.210 --> 00:59:09.920 align:middle line:90%
that you might want to consider.

00:59:09.920 --> 00:59:13.810 align:middle line:84%
The important thing is that they
are proportional to quantity.

00:59:13.810 --> 00:59:17.870 align:middle line:84%
And that's something
which also can be debated.

00:59:17.870 --> 00:59:19.540 align:middle line:84%
So if you are a
consequentialist,

00:59:19.540 --> 00:59:22.250 align:middle line:90%
you say, if I kill 1 bird--

00:59:22.250 --> 00:59:26.740 align:middle line:84%
if I kill 1,000 birds, it's
1,000 times the cost of killing

00:59:26.740 --> 00:59:33.940 align:middle line:84%
1 bird, for example, if it's
not an endangered species.

00:59:33.940 --> 00:59:37.120 align:middle line:84%
You might say, if
I kill 1,000 birds,

00:59:37.120 --> 00:59:41.385 align:middle line:84%
the damage is 1,000
times killing 1 bird.

00:59:41.385 --> 00:59:43.930 align:middle line:90%


00:59:43.930 --> 00:59:45.880 align:middle line:84%
Some people don't
think in those ways.

00:59:45.880 --> 00:59:49.720 align:middle line:84%
Actually, people would
try to measure, say,

00:59:49.720 --> 00:59:55.930 align:middle line:84%
the damage incurred when there
is an oil spill, for example.

00:59:55.930 --> 00:59:59.200 align:middle line:84%
You see that
sometimes people are

00:59:59.200 --> 01:00:03.970 align:middle line:84%
willing to pay almost as much
to save 1 bird than to save 20

01:00:03.970 --> 01:00:09.540 align:middle line:84%
birds or 50 birds, which
morally makes no sense,

01:00:09.540 --> 01:00:12.960 align:middle line:90%
but it's a reality.

01:00:12.960 --> 01:00:15.850 align:middle line:84%
I mean, we could discuss
why it's the case, actually.

01:00:15.850 --> 01:00:18.240 align:middle line:84%
Why do people react
in those ways?

01:00:18.240 --> 01:00:20.790 align:middle line:84%
So you can criticize,
but I'm just

01:00:20.790 --> 01:00:25.614 align:middle line:84%
going to assume that people
are consequentialists.

01:00:25.614 --> 01:00:28.030 align:middle line:84%
So this is an
important slide here.

01:00:28.030 --> 01:00:29.300 align:middle line:90%
Do you have any questions?

01:00:29.300 --> 01:00:34.650 align:middle line:90%


01:00:34.650 --> 01:00:37.350 align:middle line:90%
OK, so social preferences.

01:00:37.350 --> 01:00:39.720 align:middle line:90%
So the key assumption--

01:00:39.720 --> 01:00:43.050 align:middle line:84%
[? Ian, ?] and we might add
your comment on separability--

01:00:43.050 --> 01:00:46.230 align:middle line:84%
but the key assumption
in consequentialism says,

01:00:46.230 --> 01:00:47.790 align:middle line:90%
all players'--

01:00:47.790 --> 01:00:51.420 align:middle line:84%
the suppliers, the consumers,
the workers, the investors--

01:00:51.420 --> 01:00:54.450 align:middle line:84%
perception of social
impact of their trade

01:00:54.450 --> 01:00:57.270 align:middle line:84%
is proportional to the
size of that trade.

01:00:57.270 --> 01:01:00.290 align:middle line:84%
So if you pollute twice as
much, it's twice as bad.

01:01:00.290 --> 01:01:04.840 align:middle line:90%


01:01:04.840 --> 01:01:09.910 align:middle line:84%
The second assumption, which
is going to be crucial and--

01:01:09.910 --> 01:01:11.600 align:middle line:90%
read that carefully.

01:01:11.600 --> 01:01:13.780 align:middle line:90%
What is a flexible price?

01:01:13.780 --> 01:01:18.160 align:middle line:84%
So I will say that prices are
locally flexible at equilibrium,

01:01:18.160 --> 01:01:22.020 align:middle line:90%
configuration p hat.

01:01:22.020 --> 01:01:26.370 align:middle line:84%
By saying that, if I
change my ethical behavior,

01:01:26.370 --> 01:01:31.610 align:middle line:84%
delta ai, a little bit, then I
can compensate through a price

01:01:31.610 --> 01:01:32.110 align:middle line:90%
change.

01:01:32.110 --> 01:01:34.590 align:middle line:84%
Whatever sign it
has, I can compensate

01:01:34.590 --> 01:01:38.460 align:middle line:84%
through a price change delta
pi, which has to be, of course,

01:01:38.460 --> 01:01:40.470 align:middle line:90%
minus phi prime of ai.

01:01:40.470 --> 01:01:43.650 align:middle line:84%
So it has to keep the
net price constant.

01:01:43.650 --> 01:01:48.720 align:middle line:84%
So if I change the
morality of my action,

01:01:48.720 --> 01:01:52.120 align:middle line:84%
I can have an upsetting
effect in the price.

01:01:52.120 --> 01:01:55.170 align:middle line:84%
So that overall, I don't
change the attractiveness

01:01:55.170 --> 01:01:56.560 align:middle line:90%
of my offering.

01:01:56.560 --> 01:01:59.160 align:middle line:90%


01:01:59.160 --> 01:02:04.260 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, demand remains
constant in that case.

01:02:04.260 --> 01:02:07.880 align:middle line:84%
And there will be two
important counter examples.

01:02:07.880 --> 01:02:11.020 align:middle line:84%
The first is the case where
p i is regulated, of course,

01:02:11.020 --> 01:02:13.510 align:middle line:90%
because then you cannot do that.

01:02:13.510 --> 01:02:16.030 align:middle line:84%
The second, which is more
interesting in a sense,

01:02:16.030 --> 01:02:21.910 align:middle line:84%
is the case where you
have limited liability.

01:02:21.910 --> 01:02:27.140 align:middle line:84%
So you see, if you have this
constraint which is binding,

01:02:27.140 --> 01:02:31.150 align:middle line:84%
so if your price is equal
to your marginal cost,

01:02:31.150 --> 01:02:35.110 align:middle line:84%
then you are in the
limited liability region.

01:02:35.110 --> 01:02:40.300 align:middle line:84%
And if you say, for example,
you increase the morality

01:02:40.300 --> 01:02:45.110 align:middle line:84%
of your action, that costs
you more money, for example.

01:02:45.110 --> 01:02:51.300 align:middle line:84%
Then you have to raise
your price accordingly.

01:02:51.300 --> 01:02:54.860 align:middle line:90%


01:02:54.860 --> 01:02:58.840 align:middle line:84%
And that may not actually
be neutral with respect

01:02:58.840 --> 01:02:59.860 align:middle line:90%
to the demand curve.

01:02:59.860 --> 01:03:02.650 align:middle line:84%
So there is a link
here that introduces

01:03:02.650 --> 01:03:05.360 align:middle line:84%
a direct link between the
price and the moral action.

01:03:05.360 --> 01:03:08.680 align:middle line:84%
And same thing-- if you are
not-for-profit, basically,

01:03:08.680 --> 01:03:12.040 align:middle line:84%
you have to obey p
i equals ci of ai.

01:03:12.040 --> 01:03:17.230 align:middle line:84%
And again, you don't
have flexible prices.

01:03:17.230 --> 01:03:20.191 align:middle line:90%
Otherwise, you do.

01:03:20.191 --> 01:03:23.440 align:middle line:84%
And finally, we are going
to assume constant returns

01:03:23.440 --> 01:03:24.740 align:middle line:90%
to scale of a certain kind.

01:03:24.740 --> 01:03:27.490 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to assume
that the cost function--

01:03:27.490 --> 01:03:29.140 align:middle line:90%
I already did that--

01:03:29.140 --> 01:03:33.052 align:middle line:84%
is basically-- I mean, you could
have decreasing or increasing

01:03:33.052 --> 01:03:33.760 align:middle line:90%
returns to scale.

01:03:33.760 --> 01:03:38.650 align:middle line:84%
But at least with respect to the
moral action, that has to be--

01:03:38.650 --> 01:03:40.890 align:middle line:84%
basically, you have
constant return to scale.

01:03:40.890 --> 01:03:44.147 align:middle line:90%


01:03:44.147 --> 01:03:46.730 align:middle line:84%
Actually, there are a bunch of
cases where you can generalize.

01:03:46.730 --> 01:03:49.540 align:middle line:84%
So this assumption is not
as strong as it looks.

01:03:49.540 --> 01:03:53.330 align:middle line:90%


01:03:53.330 --> 01:03:56.900 align:middle line:90%
Those are the assumptions.

01:03:56.900 --> 01:04:01.140 align:middle line:84%
The first thing is
that, if you fix

01:04:01.140 --> 01:04:04.920 align:middle line:84%
prices, which will be the case
when prices are regulated--

01:04:04.920 --> 01:04:08.880 align:middle line:84%
if you fix prices, then
the ethical choices

01:04:08.880 --> 01:04:13.430 align:middle line:84%
are strategic complements
under weak conditions.

01:04:13.430 --> 01:04:17.590 align:middle line:84%
So that's going to
be true if consumers

01:04:17.590 --> 01:04:21.282 align:middle line:90%
are socially irresponsible.

01:04:21.282 --> 01:04:24.640 align:middle line:84%
So that means that demand
decreases with the morality

01:04:24.640 --> 01:04:26.790 align:middle line:90%
of the action.

01:04:26.790 --> 01:04:30.130 align:middle line:84%
That's always going to be true
if the economy is symmetrical.

01:04:30.130 --> 01:04:34.140 align:middle line:84%
So if you have a symmetric
economy, and you--

01:04:34.140 --> 01:04:42.260 align:middle line:84%
or if the social preferences
are not too strong.

01:04:42.260 --> 01:04:45.390 align:middle line:90%


01:04:45.390 --> 01:04:48.845 align:middle line:84%
I mean, you have a first factor
for strategic complementarity

01:04:48.845 --> 01:04:50.220 align:middle line:84%
in strategy
[INAUDIBLE], which is

01:04:50.220 --> 01:04:52.740 align:middle line:84%
that we have assumed
strategic complementarity

01:04:52.740 --> 01:04:55.290 align:middle line:90%
in the first place on prices.

01:04:55.290 --> 01:04:58.710 align:middle line:84%
But since prices are
like ethical choices,

01:04:58.710 --> 01:05:06.460 align:middle line:84%
you see, that translates into
strategic complementarity for--

01:05:06.460 --> 01:05:12.880 align:middle line:84%
So that tells you
that you may easily

01:05:12.880 --> 01:05:16.090 align:middle line:84%
have multiple equilibria,
or multiple social norms

01:05:16.090 --> 01:05:21.040 align:middle line:84%
in ethical behavior, if
prices are fixed exogenously,

01:05:21.040 --> 01:05:25.052 align:middle line:84%
if they are regulated,
which is not--

01:05:25.052 --> 01:05:26.760 align:middle line:84%
that's going to make
things interesting--

01:05:26.760 --> 01:05:28.740 align:middle line:84%
which is not going
to be the case at all

01:05:28.740 --> 01:05:30.815 align:middle line:90%
if prices are flexible.

01:05:30.815 --> 01:05:32.940 align:middle line:84%
Actually, we are going to
see that there are always

01:05:32.940 --> 01:05:36.380 align:middle line:84%
uniquely determined if
prices are flexible.

01:05:36.380 --> 01:05:40.250 align:middle line:84%
So if prices are fixed, you
can have multiple social norms

01:05:40.250 --> 01:05:42.560 align:middle line:90%
about moral behavior.

01:05:42.560 --> 01:05:44.240 align:middle line:84%
But when they are
flexible, we will

01:05:44.240 --> 01:05:46.080 align:middle line:84%
see there is only
one possible norm.

01:05:46.080 --> 01:05:50.290 align:middle line:90%


01:05:50.290 --> 01:05:56.280 align:middle line:84%
Now, here is the first order
condition with respect to ai.

01:05:56.280 --> 01:05:59.890 align:middle line:90%
Actually, I don't know if--

01:05:59.890 --> 01:06:01.100 align:middle line:90%
you have seen that.

01:06:01.100 --> 01:06:05.740 align:middle line:84%
You may not remember, but you
have seen that many times.

01:06:05.740 --> 01:06:15.390 align:middle line:84%
If you have a standard
oligopolist with alpha i equals

01:06:15.390 --> 01:06:17.460 align:middle line:84%
0-- so there is no
more preferences,

01:06:17.460 --> 01:06:20.890 align:middle line:84%
which is what you study
in [14.]271 usually--

01:06:20.890 --> 01:06:25.000 align:middle line:84%
then you have Lerner
index equals to 1

01:06:25.000 --> 01:06:27.760 align:middle line:84%
over the elasticity
of demand, where

01:06:27.760 --> 01:06:30.505 align:middle line:84%
the Lerner index is p
i minus phi i over p

01:06:30.505 --> 01:06:33.170 align:middle line:90%
i, the price cost margin.

01:06:33.170 --> 01:06:35.690 align:middle line:84%
That's true for a monopolist,
but that's true also

01:06:35.690 --> 01:06:36.530 align:middle line:90%
for an oligopolist.

01:06:36.530 --> 01:06:39.350 align:middle line:84%
Of course, the elasticity
is not the same--

01:06:39.350 --> 01:06:42.920 align:middle line:84%
elasticity of demand is not
the same for an oligopolist

01:06:42.920 --> 01:06:43.920 align:middle line:90%
and for a monopolist.

01:06:43.920 --> 01:06:46.550 align:middle line:90%
But this formula is always true.

01:06:46.550 --> 01:06:50.330 align:middle line:84%
That's just the first order
condition with respect to price.

01:06:50.330 --> 01:06:54.650 align:middle line:84%
Now, for the moment, I'm
not maximizing with respect

01:06:54.650 --> 01:06:55.880 align:middle line:90%
to price.

01:06:55.880 --> 01:07:00.370 align:middle line:84%
I'm maximizing with
respect to the action.

01:07:00.370 --> 01:07:02.900 align:middle line:90%


01:07:02.900 --> 01:07:06.620 align:middle line:84%
Just do the computation
from the objective function.

01:07:06.620 --> 01:07:08.580 align:middle line:90%
And what is that?

01:07:08.580 --> 01:07:11.480 align:middle line:84%
So the left-hand side-- so the
right-hand side is something

01:07:11.480 --> 01:07:13.100 align:middle line:90%
which is pretty well known--

01:07:13.100 --> 01:07:16.630 align:middle line:84%
product of the
Lerner index times

01:07:16.630 --> 01:07:18.950 align:middle line:84%
eta i, except we are
going to see the Lerner

01:07:18.950 --> 01:07:21.230 align:middle line:90%
index is a bit different.

01:07:21.230 --> 01:07:23.540 align:middle line:84%
And this is basically,
when you change

01:07:23.540 --> 01:07:28.890 align:middle line:84%
ai, you have this
internalization

01:07:28.890 --> 01:07:34.410 align:middle line:84%
of your own actions'
impact, Wi of a prime,

01:07:34.410 --> 01:07:36.230 align:middle line:90%
minus your marginal cost.

01:07:36.230 --> 01:07:38.900 align:middle line:90%


01:07:38.900 --> 01:07:42.140 align:middle line:84%
And this phi i prime is
just a normalization,

01:07:42.140 --> 01:07:47.027 align:middle line:84%
in a sense, that transforms
moral action into money.

01:07:47.027 --> 01:07:48.610 align:middle line:84%
That's a marginal
rate of substitution

01:07:48.610 --> 01:07:54.250 align:middle line:84%
between the moral action
and money for the consumer.

01:07:54.250 --> 01:07:57.730 align:middle line:90%
So that's the equation.

01:07:57.730 --> 01:08:04.360 align:middle line:84%
But an interesting thing is the
expression of the Lerner index.

01:08:04.360 --> 01:08:06.520 align:middle line:84%
So as I mentioned,
the Lerner index

01:08:06.520 --> 01:08:09.130 align:middle line:84%
is always price minus
cost of a price.

01:08:09.130 --> 01:08:15.060 align:middle line:84%
If you have social
preferences, the cost

01:08:15.060 --> 01:08:17.910 align:middle line:84%
is a modified cost,
which includes

01:08:17.910 --> 01:08:21.240 align:middle line:90%
your social responsibility.

01:08:21.240 --> 01:08:24.600 align:middle line:84%
Multiply it by alpha i,
which is your internalization

01:08:24.600 --> 01:08:26.800 align:middle line:90%
of social welfare.

01:08:26.800 --> 01:08:31.960 align:middle line:84%
And the social responsibility
index, in a sense,

01:08:31.960 --> 01:08:35.649 align:middle line:84%
has a very simple
expression, which

01:08:35.649 --> 01:08:41.020 align:middle line:84%
reflects what happens when
you change your moral action

01:08:41.020 --> 01:08:43.779 align:middle line:90%
or you change your price.

01:08:43.779 --> 01:08:48.130 align:middle line:84%
Here, I forgot to say that
wi is equal to Wi of ai

01:08:48.130 --> 01:08:51.310 align:middle line:90%
and wj is equal to Wj of aj.

01:08:51.310 --> 01:08:55.504 align:middle line:90%


01:08:55.504 --> 01:09:03.029 align:middle line:84%
And sigma ij is basically
the displacement.

01:09:03.029 --> 01:09:08.720 align:middle line:84%
So for example, if I
acquire one consumer,

01:09:08.720 --> 01:09:11.430 align:middle line:84%
the question is, where does
this consumer come from?

01:09:11.430 --> 01:09:12.890 align:middle line:90%
It can come from--

01:09:12.890 --> 01:09:17.870 align:middle line:84%
either from j or from
the outside option,

01:09:17.870 --> 01:09:20.550 align:middle line:84%
because this consumer
was not consuming before.

01:09:20.550 --> 01:09:22.399 align:middle line:84%
That's why we have
j greater than 0

01:09:22.399 --> 01:09:24.380 align:middle line:90%
to include the outside option.

01:09:24.380 --> 01:09:28.430 align:middle line:84%
And what matters is a
difference in terms of welfare

01:09:28.430 --> 01:09:31.700 align:middle line:90%
consequences between i and j.

01:09:31.700 --> 01:09:36.109 align:middle line:84%
And the sum of sigma
ij is equal to 1.

01:09:36.109 --> 01:09:39.635 align:middle line:90%
So it's a weighted--

01:09:39.635 --> 01:09:41.555 align:middle line:90%
you look at-- you ask--

01:09:41.555 --> 01:09:44.060 align:middle line:84%
it's really about the
replacement effect.

01:09:44.060 --> 01:09:48.819 align:middle line:84%
You look at, if I acquire
one more consumer,

01:09:48.819 --> 01:09:51.770 align:middle line:84%
am I going to increase
pollution or reduce pollution?

01:09:51.770 --> 01:09:57.580 align:middle line:84%
Well, that depends where
I get this consumer from.

01:09:57.580 --> 01:10:02.440 align:middle line:84%
So if I get it from someone
who was producing using coal,

01:10:02.440 --> 01:10:06.350 align:middle line:84%
that's a good thing,
even if I use gas.

01:10:06.350 --> 01:10:10.550 align:middle line:84%
But if I get this
consumer from someone

01:10:10.550 --> 01:10:12.620 align:middle line:84%
who was actually
using renewables,

01:10:12.620 --> 01:10:17.200 align:middle line:84%
that's a bad thing
if I'm using gas.

01:10:17.200 --> 01:10:21.250 align:middle line:84%
So basically, you look
at the impact on welfare.

01:10:21.250 --> 01:10:26.265 align:middle line:84%
But for that, you have to know,
whom are you stealing consumers

01:10:26.265 --> 01:10:26.765 align:middle line:90%
from?

01:10:26.765 --> 01:10:32.580 align:middle line:90%


01:10:32.580 --> 01:10:35.480 align:middle line:84%
So that's why, in
your course, you also

01:10:35.480 --> 01:10:37.940 align:middle line:84%
take into account your
social responsibility, which

01:10:37.940 --> 01:10:43.840 align:middle line:84%
means that if you are
highly polluting--

01:10:43.840 --> 01:10:45.700 align:middle line:84%
actually, your
social responsibility

01:10:45.700 --> 01:10:50.750 align:middle line:84%
is actually to leave the
activity to other firms.

01:10:50.750 --> 01:10:54.320 align:middle line:84%
If you are highly ethical,
your social responsibility

01:10:54.320 --> 01:10:59.960 align:middle line:84%
is to take market, to increase
your market share, which kind of

01:10:59.960 --> 01:11:02.600 align:middle line:90%
makes sense.

01:11:02.600 --> 01:11:05.280 align:middle line:84%
Now, if you have a
symmetric equilibrium--

01:11:05.280 --> 01:11:07.930 align:middle line:90%


01:11:07.930 --> 01:11:10.330 align:middle line:84%
if you have a
symmetric equilibrium,

01:11:10.330 --> 01:11:12.230 align:middle line:90%
then wi is equal to wj.

01:11:12.230 --> 01:11:14.000 align:middle line:84%
Let's assume that the
market is covered,

01:11:14.000 --> 01:11:16.300 align:middle line:90%
so the outside option is not--

01:11:16.300 --> 01:11:18.610 align:middle line:84%
nobody chooses the
outside option.

01:11:18.610 --> 01:11:21.370 align:middle line:90%
Everybody needs a doctor.

01:11:21.370 --> 01:11:24.790 align:middle line:84%
And basically, then
wi is equal to wj,

01:11:24.790 --> 01:11:28.390 align:middle line:84%
and then your social
responsibility is 0

01:11:28.390 --> 01:11:31.350 align:middle line:90%
because you're all the same.

01:11:31.350 --> 01:11:34.200 align:middle line:84%
In that case, the Lerner index
is a standard Lerner index

01:11:34.200 --> 01:11:36.380 align:middle line:84%
if you have a
symmetric equilibrium.

01:11:36.380 --> 01:11:41.580 align:middle line:84%
But the basic point is that,
under a pretty weak assumption,

01:11:41.580 --> 01:11:45.540 align:middle line:90%
then you get multiple--

01:11:45.540 --> 01:11:50.100 align:middle line:84%
for given prices, you get,
often, multiple ethical norms

01:11:50.100 --> 01:11:51.330 align:middle line:90%
in equilibrium.

01:11:51.330 --> 01:11:57.750 align:middle line:84%
That's easy to get because of
the strategic complementarities.

01:11:57.750 --> 01:12:01.410 align:middle line:84%
Whereas, when you look
at flexible prices,

01:12:01.410 --> 01:12:05.220 align:middle line:84%
you get a very
different picture, which

01:12:05.220 --> 01:12:10.500 align:middle line:84%
is that, if you have
flexible prices,

01:12:10.500 --> 01:12:13.350 align:middle line:84%
your moral behavior
is never going

01:12:13.350 --> 01:12:16.300 align:middle line:90%
to depend on the demand curve.

01:12:16.300 --> 01:12:18.700 align:middle line:84%
So it's going to be the
same for a monopolist

01:12:18.700 --> 01:12:21.250 align:middle line:90%
or for an oligopolist.

01:12:21.250 --> 01:12:26.200 align:middle line:84%
It's the same for an
oligopoly and a cartel.

01:12:26.200 --> 01:12:30.190 align:middle line:84%
The demand curve, whether they
are highly substitutable or not,

01:12:30.190 --> 01:12:31.870 align:middle line:90%
doesn't matter.

01:12:31.870 --> 01:12:37.430 align:middle line:84%
So if prices are
flexible, actually,

01:12:37.430 --> 01:12:40.610 align:middle line:84%
then competitive
pressure doesn't matter.

01:12:40.610 --> 01:12:43.370 align:middle line:84%
Which is important
because it tells you,

01:12:43.370 --> 01:12:46.620 align:middle line:84%
just go ahead with
competition policy.

01:12:46.620 --> 01:12:48.670 align:middle line:90%
Those are two separate things.

01:12:48.670 --> 01:12:52.710 align:middle line:84%
Moral behavior will
depend on how moral

01:12:52.710 --> 01:12:57.090 align:middle line:84%
the stakeholders are but will
not depend on the demand curve,

01:12:57.090 --> 01:13:01.310 align:middle line:84%
so will not depend on the
intensity of competition.

01:13:01.310 --> 01:13:05.575 align:middle line:84%
So actually, in
that case, I mean--

01:13:05.575 --> 01:13:10.410 align:middle line:90%


01:13:10.410 --> 01:13:18.210 align:middle line:84%
so put differently, if you
have a price optimization,

01:13:18.210 --> 01:13:22.570 align:middle line:84%
you know that you're
going to get this.

01:13:22.570 --> 01:13:26.170 align:middle line:84%
Price will be equal to the
Lerner index, perhaps modified

01:13:26.170 --> 01:13:27.560 align:middle line:90%
by the social responsibility.

01:13:27.560 --> 01:13:33.354 align:middle line:84%
But it's always the case that
if you have price competition,

01:13:33.354 --> 01:13:37.990 align:middle line:84%
the Lerner index is equal to the
inverse elasticity of demand.

01:13:37.990 --> 01:13:40.040 align:middle line:90%
So that will be equal to 1.

01:13:40.040 --> 01:13:46.610 align:middle line:84%
And basically, this entity
will be equal to 1 as well.

01:13:46.610 --> 01:13:49.210 align:middle line:84%
And basically, your
moral behavior,

01:13:49.210 --> 01:13:51.940 align:middle line:84%
indexed by [INAUDIBLE],
will be given

01:13:51.940 --> 01:13:55.480 align:middle line:84%
by this equation,
which means that it's

01:13:55.480 --> 01:13:57.345 align:middle line:84%
completely independent
of the demand curve.

01:13:57.345 --> 01:14:01.070 align:middle line:90%


01:14:01.070 --> 01:14:07.070 align:middle line:84%
So whenever prices are flexible,
the intensity of competition

01:14:07.070 --> 01:14:09.490 align:middle line:90%
is completely irrelevant.

01:14:09.490 --> 01:14:14.527 align:middle line:84%
And then therefore, there is
no point not doing antitrust

01:14:14.527 --> 01:14:16.110 align:middle line:84%
because you want to
increase morality,

01:14:16.110 --> 01:14:19.670 align:middle line:84%
because you're not going to
increase morality anyway.

01:14:19.670 --> 01:14:22.280 align:middle line:90%
It's going to remain constant.

01:14:22.280 --> 01:14:22.790 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:14:22.790 --> 01:14:25.160 align:middle line:90%
What is intuition?

01:14:25.160 --> 01:14:29.870 align:middle line:84%
Let me give you two intuitions
for whatever they are worth.

01:14:29.870 --> 01:14:36.260 align:middle line:84%
The first is to understand there
are two offsetting effects.

01:14:36.260 --> 01:14:40.040 align:middle line:90%
So takes a particular case.

01:14:40.040 --> 01:14:42.590 align:middle line:84%
So we are going to take
a particular case, which

01:14:42.590 --> 01:14:45.080 align:middle line:90%
is a worry of many people--

01:14:45.080 --> 01:14:48.040 align:middle line:84%
the fact-- the
replacement effect.

01:14:48.040 --> 01:14:53.050 align:middle line:84%
So the idea is that
I might want to bribe

01:14:53.050 --> 01:14:56.160 align:middle line:84%
an official because, otherwise,
I will lose the market.

01:14:56.160 --> 01:14:59.210 align:middle line:90%
So irresponsible consumers.

01:14:59.210 --> 01:15:03.570 align:middle line:84%
So with irresponsible consumers,
if I cut ethical corner,

01:15:03.570 --> 01:15:08.170 align:middle line:84%
so if I reduce my morality,
I gain market share

01:15:08.170 --> 01:15:12.730 align:middle line:84%
because that's exactly what the
corrupt official, the dictator,

01:15:12.730 --> 01:15:16.070 align:middle line:90%
or the opioid consumer wants.

01:15:16.070 --> 01:15:20.905 align:middle line:84%
So if I behave less morally,
I gain market share.

01:15:20.905 --> 01:15:24.260 align:middle line:90%


01:15:24.260 --> 01:15:27.680 align:middle line:84%
Now, if you change the
elasticity of demand,

01:15:27.680 --> 01:15:29.510 align:middle line:84%
so you increase the
elasticity of demand

01:15:29.510 --> 01:15:32.570 align:middle line:84%
by having more
competition, then that

01:15:32.570 --> 01:15:38.800 align:middle line:84%
means that I'm going to get
more market share from--

01:15:38.800 --> 01:15:41.410 align:middle line:84%
I will get a bigger
increase in market share

01:15:41.410 --> 01:15:45.210 align:middle line:90%
if I behave less morally.

01:15:45.210 --> 01:15:48.220 align:middle line:84%
So that seems to confirm
the conventional wisdom

01:15:48.220 --> 01:15:51.610 align:middle line:84%
that, actually, with the
responsible consumer,

01:15:51.610 --> 01:15:54.760 align:middle line:84%
markets will lead
to immoral behavior.

01:15:54.760 --> 01:15:59.440 align:middle line:84%
Because then, by
cutting ethical corners,

01:15:59.440 --> 01:16:02.560 align:middle line:84%
you actually gain a
lot of market share.

01:16:02.560 --> 01:16:07.870 align:middle line:84%
But at the same time, if
you have more competition,

01:16:07.870 --> 01:16:10.690 align:middle line:90%
you also have lower markups.

01:16:10.690 --> 01:16:14.520 align:middle line:84%
And if you have
lower markups, that

01:16:14.520 --> 01:16:17.160 align:middle line:84%
means you gain less
in terms of money

01:16:17.160 --> 01:16:20.010 align:middle line:84%
whenever you acquire
market share.

01:16:20.010 --> 01:16:23.070 align:middle line:84%
And that makes-- you have
a tradeoff between behaving

01:16:23.070 --> 01:16:25.070 align:middle line:90%
morally and making money.

01:16:25.070 --> 01:16:27.898 align:middle line:90%


01:16:27.898 --> 01:16:33.030 align:middle line:84%
But when you behave immorally,
you make less money per unit

01:16:33.030 --> 01:16:35.190 align:middle line:90%
on more units in a sense.

01:16:35.190 --> 01:16:39.070 align:middle line:84%
And those two effects
actually perfectly offset.

01:16:39.070 --> 01:16:40.860 align:middle line:84%
That, of course,
I'm not showing you.

01:16:40.860 --> 01:16:43.590 align:middle line:84%
I'm just showing you there
are two effects which

01:16:43.590 --> 01:16:47.290 align:middle line:90%
go in the opposite directions.

01:16:47.290 --> 01:16:51.820 align:middle line:84%
I mean, if everybody
made 0 at the margin,

01:16:51.820 --> 01:16:54.480 align:middle line:84%
nobody would have incentive
to behave immorally.

01:16:54.480 --> 01:16:57.310 align:middle line:90%


01:16:57.310 --> 01:17:00.490 align:middle line:84%
If you don't make any
money from misbehaving,

01:17:00.490 --> 01:17:03.400 align:middle line:84%
then you'll behave morally if
you have any social preferences,

01:17:03.400 --> 01:17:04.440 align:middle line:90%
even weak ones.

01:17:04.440 --> 01:17:07.241 align:middle line:90%


01:17:07.241 --> 01:17:11.300 align:middle line:84%
And competition is going to
reduce the stake, in a sense.

01:17:11.300 --> 01:17:14.230 align:middle line:84%
So it's going to push
toward more moral behavior.

01:17:14.230 --> 01:17:17.720 align:middle line:84%
So you have two effects,
and they offset.

01:17:17.720 --> 01:17:21.516 align:middle line:84%
The other way to see it is
some kind of cost minimization.

01:17:21.516 --> 01:17:25.028 align:middle line:90%


01:17:25.028 --> 01:17:28.230 align:middle line:84%
And that explains why they
perfectly offset, in a sense.

01:17:28.230 --> 01:17:31.230 align:middle line:84%
So when you look at your
costs as a function of ai,

01:17:31.230 --> 01:17:37.900 align:middle line:84%
ci of ai, but then you also
have to add phi i of ai.

01:17:37.900 --> 01:17:42.580 align:middle line:84%
Because phi i of ai is basically
the perceived price increase

01:17:42.580 --> 01:17:46.980 align:middle line:90%
due to ai for the consumer.

01:17:46.980 --> 01:17:49.820 align:middle line:84%
So if you want to keep
your demand constant,

01:17:49.820 --> 01:17:53.215 align:middle line:84%
you have to reduce your
price by phi i of ai.

01:17:53.215 --> 01:17:56.090 align:middle line:90%


01:17:56.090 --> 01:17:58.930 align:middle line:84%
And then there is your
social responsibility,

01:17:58.930 --> 01:18:01.060 align:middle line:90%
which we wrote here.

01:18:01.060 --> 01:18:07.150 align:middle line:84%
So remember, sigma ij is
the fraction of consumers

01:18:07.150 --> 01:18:09.970 align:middle line:84%
that you steal from
firm j whenever

01:18:09.970 --> 01:18:13.645 align:middle line:90%
you acquire new consumers.

01:18:13.645 --> 01:18:16.360 align:middle line:84%
And then I rewrote,
slightly differently,

01:18:16.360 --> 01:18:17.840 align:middle line:90%
the objective function.

01:18:17.840 --> 01:18:20.230 align:middle line:84%
So I have to take into
account the difference

01:18:20.230 --> 01:18:24.340 align:middle line:84%
between the welfare that I
offer and the welfare which

01:18:24.340 --> 01:18:27.946 align:middle line:90%
is offered by my competitors.

01:18:27.946 --> 01:18:33.550 align:middle line:84%
And when you look at this,
given that sum of sigma ij

01:18:33.550 --> 01:18:38.530 align:middle line:90%
is equal to 1, you can--

01:18:38.530 --> 01:18:41.980 align:middle line:84%
you can rewrite it this
way, in a part which--

01:18:41.980 --> 01:18:50.140 align:middle line:84%
as a part which depends on my
own action and another part

01:18:50.140 --> 01:18:53.620 align:middle line:84%
which depends on the
prices, the net prices,

01:18:53.620 --> 01:18:56.980 align:middle line:84%
but also on the
action of the rivals,

01:18:56.980 --> 01:18:58.870 align:middle line:90%
including the outside option.

01:18:58.870 --> 01:19:01.660 align:middle line:90%
And it's completely separable.

01:19:01.660 --> 01:19:05.275 align:middle line:84%
So that means that, OK, that
I cannot do anything about.

01:19:05.275 --> 01:19:08.980 align:middle line:90%


01:19:08.980 --> 01:19:13.460 align:middle line:84%
For given net prices, I
can just minimize costs.

01:19:13.460 --> 01:19:16.905 align:middle line:84%
And that gives me the formula,
which is on the previous page.

01:19:16.905 --> 01:19:21.548 align:middle line:90%


01:19:21.548 --> 01:19:27.570 align:middle line:90%
So that's a proof.

01:19:27.570 --> 01:19:32.610 align:middle line:84%
Now, how robust is this
conclusion of irrelevance?

01:19:32.610 --> 01:19:36.930 align:middle line:84%
It's not fully robust, but
it's actually pretty robust--

01:19:36.930 --> 01:19:38.970 align:middle line:90%
more than we would have thought.

01:19:38.970 --> 01:19:44.190 align:middle line:84%
So for example,
the relevance of--

01:19:44.190 --> 01:19:46.950 align:middle line:84%
again, the relevance is that
the intensity of competition

01:19:46.950 --> 01:19:54.540 align:middle line:84%
has no impact on the choice of
morality if prices are flexible.

01:19:54.540 --> 01:19:55.920 align:middle line:90%
You can use Cournot.

01:19:55.920 --> 01:19:58.030 align:middle line:84%
In that case, you
don't choose a price.

01:19:58.030 --> 01:20:00.590 align:middle line:90%
You choose a quantity.

01:20:00.590 --> 01:20:02.790 align:middle line:84%
But that's still
the case that works.

01:20:02.790 --> 01:20:04.110 align:middle line:90%
You can use a search model.

01:20:04.110 --> 01:20:06.710 align:middle line:90%
It also works.

01:20:06.710 --> 01:20:11.540 align:middle line:84%
If you have imperfect consumer
information, that works.

01:20:11.540 --> 01:20:17.340 align:middle line:84%
If you have different
internalizations,

01:20:17.340 --> 01:20:20.060 align:middle line:84%
I give you a specific
internalization,

01:20:20.060 --> 01:20:22.370 align:middle line:84%
you can generalize
to nonlinear pricing.

01:20:22.370 --> 01:20:24.080 align:middle line:84%
So I've assumed
you need demands,

01:20:24.080 --> 01:20:26.300 align:middle line:84%
but you could have
multiunit demands.

01:20:26.300 --> 01:20:30.200 align:middle line:90%
That will also work and so on.

01:20:30.200 --> 01:20:34.190 align:middle line:84%
Non-constant returns to scale
is important for the result,

01:20:34.190 --> 01:20:37.170 align:middle line:90%
but you can relax it somewhat.

01:20:37.170 --> 01:20:43.890 align:middle line:84%
So, for example, if ethics
impacts only the demand

01:20:43.890 --> 01:20:47.080 align:middle line:84%
side but not the cost
side, it doesn't matter.

01:20:47.080 --> 01:20:49.680 align:middle line:84%
You could have decreasing
or increasing return

01:20:49.680 --> 01:20:50.940 align:middle line:90%
to scale and so on.

01:20:50.940 --> 01:20:55.020 align:middle line:90%
So it's pretty robust.

01:20:55.020 --> 01:20:59.140 align:middle line:90%
Where things are different--

01:20:59.140 --> 01:21:02.760 align:middle line:84%
and probably I will
stop here because--

01:21:02.760 --> 01:21:07.760 align:middle line:90%
is if you have--

01:21:07.760 --> 01:21:10.070 align:middle line:84%
if you don't have
flexible prices,

01:21:10.070 --> 01:21:13.210 align:middle line:90%
then it's a different ball game.

01:21:13.210 --> 01:21:17.800 align:middle line:84%
So actually, it suggests
lots of empirical tests.

01:21:17.800 --> 01:21:19.660 align:middle line:84%
There are actually a
couple of experiments

01:21:19.660 --> 01:21:24.400 align:middle line:84%
which have been run which fit
relatively well with the theory,

01:21:24.400 --> 01:21:26.320 align:middle line:90%
but it's only two experiments.

01:21:26.320 --> 01:21:28.690 align:middle line:84%
We need more experiments,
and we need also

01:21:28.690 --> 01:21:32.090 align:middle line:84%
some empirical work, in general,
to check all those things.

01:21:32.090 --> 01:21:34.730 align:middle line:90%


01:21:34.730 --> 01:21:38.140 align:middle line:90%
So what we do is--

01:21:38.140 --> 01:21:42.880 align:middle line:84%
OK, let's assume, first,
you have a regulated price.

01:21:42.880 --> 01:21:44.620 align:middle line:84%
If you have a
regulated price, you

01:21:44.620 --> 01:21:49.930 align:middle line:84%
have no reduced-markup effects
of intense competition.

01:21:49.930 --> 01:21:54.550 align:middle line:84%
Competition reduces markup, and
therefore reduces the stakes

01:21:54.550 --> 01:21:57.230 align:middle line:90%
in behaving immorally.

01:21:57.230 --> 01:21:59.355 align:middle line:84%
Whereas if you
have fixed prices,

01:21:59.355 --> 01:22:00.480 align:middle line:90%
that's not going to happen.

01:22:00.480 --> 01:22:03.175 align:middle line:84%
Competition is not going
to reduce the price.

01:22:03.175 --> 01:22:05.760 align:middle line:90%


01:22:05.760 --> 01:22:09.000 align:middle line:84%
In that case, the
critiques of markets

01:22:09.000 --> 01:22:13.560 align:middle line:84%
are right, which is, if you
have irresponsible consumers,

01:22:13.560 --> 01:22:17.410 align:middle line:84%
then more competition is going
to reduce moral behavior.

01:22:17.410 --> 01:22:19.810 align:middle line:90%
That's correct.

01:22:19.810 --> 01:22:22.870 align:middle line:84%
So if you make the assumption
there is a unique equilibrium,

01:22:22.870 --> 01:22:26.530 align:middle line:84%
you can show that actually
more behavior decreases

01:22:26.530 --> 01:22:27.830 align:middle line:90%
with more competition.

01:22:27.830 --> 01:22:30.790 align:middle line:84%
So there's a grain of
truth in the criticism.

01:22:30.790 --> 01:22:32.570 align:middle line:84%
It's not like it's
completely wrong.

01:22:32.570 --> 01:22:34.180 align:middle line:90%
There's a grain of truth.

01:22:34.180 --> 01:22:37.870 align:middle line:84%
But you have to qualify it
substantially, not only because

01:22:37.870 --> 01:22:42.160 align:middle line:84%
of the irrelevance result, but
even when the prices are fixed,

01:22:42.160 --> 01:22:46.090 align:middle line:84%
then you also get the
reverse conclusion

01:22:46.090 --> 01:22:49.370 align:middle line:84%
when you have
responsible consumers.

01:22:49.370 --> 01:22:51.500 align:middle line:84%
It's pretty clear
what happens is that,

01:22:51.500 --> 01:22:54.470 align:middle line:84%
if you have a responsible
consumer-- so we all

01:22:54.470 --> 01:22:57.090 align:middle line:90%
want fair trade coffee.

01:22:57.090 --> 01:23:00.180 align:middle line:84%
Then, actually,
more competition is

01:23:00.180 --> 01:23:03.810 align:middle line:84%
going to force
the firms actually

01:23:03.810 --> 01:23:07.530 align:middle line:84%
to buy fair trade
coffee because they

01:23:07.530 --> 01:23:09.020 align:middle line:90%
want to please the customer.

01:23:09.020 --> 01:23:11.080 align:middle line:84%
So it's going to be
exactly the reverse.

01:23:11.080 --> 01:23:13.890 align:middle line:84%
So if you have
responsible consumers,

01:23:13.890 --> 01:23:17.070 align:middle line:84%
then competition is
a good formality.

01:23:17.070 --> 01:23:20.820 align:middle line:84%
So if you add
those things, which

01:23:20.820 --> 01:23:24.860 align:middle line:84%
is the relevance
result plus this slide,

01:23:24.860 --> 01:23:31.000 align:middle line:84%
you see that, first, it's not
the case that everything goes.

01:23:31.000 --> 01:23:35.020 align:middle line:84%
You have predictions, but
they are context-specific.

01:23:35.020 --> 01:23:37.450 align:middle line:90%
Is a price flexible or not?

01:23:37.450 --> 01:23:40.860 align:middle line:84%
Are the consumers
irresponsible or responsible?

01:23:40.860 --> 01:23:44.170 align:middle line:84%
Those are not the
same applications.

01:23:44.170 --> 01:23:49.430 align:middle line:84%
And you get a picture, which
you say, OK, we cannot conclude.

01:23:49.430 --> 01:23:52.330 align:middle line:84%
And just modifying antitrust
and all those things

01:23:52.330 --> 01:23:55.210 align:middle line:84%
just on the basis of
morality doesn't seem

01:23:55.210 --> 01:23:56.830 align:middle line:90%
like the right thing to do.

01:23:56.830 --> 01:24:01.660 align:middle line:84%
Now, if the policymakers
use the [INAUDIBLE],

01:24:01.660 --> 01:24:03.650 align:middle line:84%
maybe that's the
right thing to do.

01:24:03.650 --> 01:24:09.340 align:middle line:84%
But you cannot just say,
we don't want markets.

01:24:09.340 --> 01:24:11.440 align:middle line:90%
That's not the right stance.

01:24:11.440 --> 01:24:15.080 align:middle line:84%
Now, the last thing we
do in the paper, I think,

01:24:15.080 --> 01:24:17.560 align:middle line:84%
is to look at what
happens when you

01:24:17.560 --> 01:24:24.710 align:middle line:84%
introduce either heterogeneity
in social preferences.

01:24:24.710 --> 01:24:29.128 align:middle line:84%
Imagine one of the
competitors is Mother Teresa.

01:24:29.128 --> 01:24:30.030 align:middle line:90%
What happens?

01:24:30.030 --> 01:24:34.706 align:middle line:84%
Well, she might have a
limited liability constraint.

01:24:34.706 --> 01:24:37.850 align:middle line:84%
Because at some point,
she will run out of cash.

01:24:37.850 --> 01:24:41.720 align:middle line:84%
Or if you have a
for-profit, then it

01:24:41.720 --> 01:24:43.430 align:middle line:90%
becomes more interesting.

01:24:43.430 --> 01:24:45.830 align:middle line:84%
And there might be an
argument in some industries,

01:24:45.830 --> 01:24:48.860 align:middle line:84%
actually, to protect
the not-for-profit

01:24:48.860 --> 01:24:51.103 align:middle line:84%
from the competition
of for-profit.

01:24:51.103 --> 01:24:52.520 align:middle line:84%
Because what we
show is that, when

01:24:52.520 --> 01:24:54.290 align:middle line:84%
you have very
intense competition,

01:24:54.290 --> 01:24:57.230 align:middle line:90%
you tend to have mimicking--

01:24:57.230 --> 01:25:01.220 align:middle line:84%
the behavior-- mimic between
for-profit and not-for-profit,

01:25:01.220 --> 01:25:03.770 align:middle line:90%
the distinction disappears.

01:25:03.770 --> 01:25:07.580 align:middle line:84%
Basically, the for-profit to
compete with the not-for-profit

01:25:07.580 --> 01:25:10.490 align:middle line:84%
has to choose prices
close to marginal cost.

01:25:10.490 --> 01:25:15.860 align:middle line:84%
But the not-for-profit
have to behave unethically

01:25:15.860 --> 01:25:20.910 align:middle line:84%
to keep consumers if
consumers are irresponsible.

01:25:20.910 --> 01:25:25.500 align:middle line:84%
And you see that you lose a
little bit the distinction

01:25:25.500 --> 01:25:28.740 align:middle line:84%
between not-for-profit and
for-profit in that case.

01:25:28.740 --> 01:25:31.260 align:middle line:84%
And that raises the
issue about whether you

01:25:31.260 --> 01:25:36.660 align:middle line:84%
want to reserve the hospital
market or the education market,

01:25:36.660 --> 01:25:42.190 align:middle line:84%
where moral issues can be very
important to not-for-profit.

01:25:42.190 --> 01:25:44.310 align:middle line:84%
And we don't solve this
issue, but it's actually

01:25:44.310 --> 01:25:47.120 align:middle line:84%
a very interesting
issue to study.

01:25:47.120 --> 01:26:11.000 align:middle line:90%